[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {The most common approach is the more active one, with Black choosing to pin our knight. The idea is to develop the light-squared bishop before playing ...e6. You will face this move 31% of the time. I suggest the active Bb5 here. Instead of defending passively with Be2, we pin Black's knight and threaten to capture it, doubling the black pawns.} 4.Bb5 4...Qd6 {A move to consider is definitely ...Qd6. We can't damage Black's structure here, so I propose a different plan. It usually isn't advisable to push both d4 and f4 in the Bird, but specific lines require specific countermeasures. One of the ideas is to bring the bishop back to e2 and then solidify the knight on the e5-square. Another one is to push c4 and contest the center, sometimes even pushing c5 if Black castles queenside.} 5.d4 5...a6 {If Black pushes ...a6 here, we won't capture, but rather move according to our plan. And that's to retreat and unpin the knight. Now there's a possibility of Ne5.} 6.Be2 6...Nf6 {Black does have a countermeasure, however. And that is to place the knight on the e4-square, which is the downside of playing d4. We'll castle. There's not much we can do to stop the knight.} 7.O-O 7...Ne4 {However, once Black does go for ...Ne4, we should implement one of our main ideas in this variation and go after the defender of the knight. This move also helps grab central space and potentially attack on the queenside. And that's to expand with c4.} 8.c4 8...e6 {You will most likely face Black defending with ...e6. But once we play c4, we should continue playing the queenside. That is where Black will most likely castle soon. Can you see a way to threaten something on the queenside? Firstly, let's activate the queen and target the b7-pawn.} 9.Qb3 9...O-O-O {Castling does save it, but it gives us an idea. We don't have to fight in the center anymore. We can close it to our advantage. We push and hit the queen. This gives us tempo to continue with our operations. Once we've closed the center like this, a good idea is to continue pushing.} 10.c5 10...Qd7 {Once the Black queen retreats...} 11.Qa3 {First we have to side step to a3 in order to open up the b-pawn's path. We will push and eventually try to attack on the queenside. As you can see, the Bird can be very flexible in terms of attacking on either side. You just have to know which setup to use when you anticipate Black castling queenside, like we did here with 5.d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {The most common approach is the more active one, with Black choosing to pin our knight. The idea is to develop the light-squared bishop before playing ...e6. You will face this move 31% of the time. I suggest the active Bb5 here. Instead of defending passively with Be2, we pin Black's knight and threaten to capture it, doubling the black pawns.} 4.Bb5 4...Qd6 {A move to consider is definitely ...Qd6. We can't damage Black's structure here, so I propose a different plan. It usually isn't advisable to push both d4 and f4 in the Bird, but specific lines require specific countermeasures. One of the ideas is to bring the bishop back to e2 and then solidify the knight on the e5-square. Another one is to push c4 and contest the center, sometimes even pushing c5 if Black castles queenside.} 5.d4 5...O-O-O {And they might castle queenside right away. We'll get our king into safety first. That's always a priority.} 6.O-O 6...f6 {Black goes for ...f6, which is a sensible move. They want to control the e5-square and even push ...e5. We have other plans as well. We want to push c4 here and try to break through since Black has castled long.} 7.Nbd2 7...a6 {If Black pushes ...a6 here, we have an interesting sacrifice.} 8.a4 {This is called the Fishing Pole. We sacrifice the bishop in order to open up the a-file for the rook. There is no merit to Black capturing on b5 as they will have to defend the whole game. We would find compensation quickly.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {The most common approach is the more active one, with Black choosing to pin our knight. The idea is to develop the light-squared bishop before playing ...e6. You will face this move 31% of the time. I suggest the active Bb5 here. Instead of defending passively with Be2, we pin Black's knight and threaten to capture it, doubling the black pawns.} 4.Bb5 4...Qd6 {A move to consider is definitely ...Qd6. We can't damage Black's structure here, so I propose a different plan. It usually isn't advisable to push both d4 and f4 in the Bird, but specific lines require specific countermeasures. One of the ideas is to bring the bishop back to e2 and then solidify the knight on the e5-square. Another one is to push c4 and contest the center, sometimes even pushing c5 if Black castles queenside.} 5.d4 5...O-O-O {And they might castle queenside right away. We'll get our king into safety first. That's always a priority.} 6.O-O 6...f6 {Black goes for ...f6, which is a sensible move. They want to control the e5-square and even push ...e5. We have other plans as well. We want to push c4 here and try to break through since Black has castled long.} 7.Nbd2 7...e5 {Going for ...e5 is a mistake. There is actually a tactic in here that wins us a pawn. Let's capture the knight first. This will serve a purpose very soon, as you will see.} 8.Bxc6 {Let's capture the knight first. This will serve a purpose very soon, as you will see.} 8...Qxc6 {Black will most likely recapture with the queen to keep their pawn structure intact. Do you see a tactic here? It's to take on e5 with the knight! We capture a pawn and attack Black's queen while hanging our own. If Black takes the knight, we will capture the bishop with check.} 9.Nxe5 9...Bxd1 {They take the queen instead. And we will take their queen, of course. Now the rook is under attack.} 10.Nxc6 10...bxc6 {So they basically have to capture the knight.} 11.Rxd1 {And leave us the bishop. In the end, we've managed to grab a pawn and Black's c-pawns are doubled. White is much better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {They can play the two knights system, developing the knight to f6. It's played 22% of the time. This position can sometimes transpose, but our initial response should stay the same.} 4.Bb5 {Activating the bishop to b5 is the way to go. Black often wants to develop their light-squared bishop which this move restricts as they should defend with ...Bd7. Otherwise, we are threatening to double Black's pawns. 4...Bg4 transposes to the main line.} 4...Bd7 {The most common move is to block the attack with the bishop. It does look passive, but Black shouldn't split their pawn structure.} 5.b3 {We don't have to rush with capturing the knight. Let's just prepare for the bishop to develop and wait for ...a6.} 5...a6 {Once it arrives...} 6.Bxc6 {We'll take.} 6...Bxc6 {Black recaptures with the bishop and keeps their pawn structure, but the bishop is only staring at the pawn on d5. Besides, we will use our control of the e5-square soon and get the knight there to attack the bishop.} 7.Bb2 {First, we will develop.} 7...e6 {Now it's logical for Black to continue with ...e6, opening up their bishop.} 8.Ne5 {But we will use the e5-square now. Tehnically, this is the best move-order to threaten the bishop. Otherwise, good players will play ...b6 and ...Bb7.} 8...Bd6 {If Black continues with ...Bd6, we don't even have to capture the bishop on c6. We can continue in a positonal manner for now and soon look to transform the game into an attack.} 9.O-O {Let's castle and keep our strong knight on e5.} 9...O-O {If Black castles here, it's like giving you the green light to attack.} 10.d3 {First, d3 is a solid move to open up a square for our b-knight.} 10...Nd7 {Black will most likely try to deal with our knight on e5. But moving the knight away from f6 can prove to be dangerous for them.} 11.Qg4 {As it opens up the g4-square for our queen! Suddenly, the bishop and the queen are eyeing the g7-pawn.} 11...Nxe5 {Black has to trade here not to let our bishop come alive.} 12.fxe5 {We will recapture with the pawn. This forces the bishop on d6 to move and gives us another tempo to continue our attack.} 12...Be7 {Once it does move...} 13.Rf3 {I suggest a rook lift here. Our rook is coming to g3 to threaten the king, after which we will develop the knight and bring the a-rook to the game. Black can't play ...Bf6 to defend and their position is cramped. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {They can play the two knights system, developing the knight to f6. It's played 22% of the time. This position can sometimes transpose, but our initial response should stay the same.} 4.Bb5 {Activating the bishop to b5 is the way to go. Black often wants to develop their light-squared bishop which this move restricts as they should defend with ...Bd7. Otherwise, we are threatening to double Black's pawns. 4...Bg4 transposes to the main line.} 4...Bd7 {The most common move is to block the attack with the bishop. It does look passive, but Black shouldn't split their pawn structure.} 5.b3 {We don't have to rush with capturing the knight. Let's just prepare for the bishop to develop and wait for ...a6.} 5...a6 {Once it arrives...} 6.Bxc6 {We'll take.} 6...Bxc6 {Black recaptures with the bishop and keeps their pawn structure, but the bishop is only staring at the pawn on d5. Besides, we will use our control of the e5-square soon and get the knight there to attack the bishop.} 7.Bb2 {First, we will develop.} 7...e6 {Now it's logical for Black to continue with ...e6, opening up their bishop.} 8.Ne5 {But we will use the e5-square now. Tehnically, this is the best move-order to threaten the bishop. Otherwise, good players will play ...b6 and ...Bb7.} 8...Bb5 {Black can try to preserve the bishop, but that only leads to us getting more free tempi and expanding while chasing it.} 9.a4 {And we will do that with a4 here.} 9...Bd7 {Once the bishop retreats, we don't have to capture it. Our knight on e5 is stronger than that bishop. We don't even have to play solidly; we can continue with an active approach that could eventually lead to an attack.} 10.Qf3 {And that is because Black's setup is passive. We want to play g4 and g5 now. Our king is actually safe on e1 since we have a lot of space.} 10...Bd6 {Black can try to activate their pieces, but won't get far. We don't mind them capturing on e5.} 11.g4 {We'll just continue with g4, threatening g5. Grabbing even more space on the kingside.} 11...Bxe5 {If Black captures, it is actually better to recapture with the pawn.} 12.fxe5 {This forces one of Black's most important defenders to move, which is the knight on f6.} 12...Ng8 {Extremely passive. Black doesn't have a single active piece while our position is blossoming.} 13.h4 {When the g-pawn advances, you can continue with the h-pawn! This type of pawn storm signifies an attack incoming. It's extremely dangerous for Black to castle kingside.} 13...Ne7 {They will probably try to develop the knight to e7, with the idea of bringing it to g6.} 14.h5 {But we will shut that down by playing h5. Now the g6-square is covered.} 14...c5 {Black doesn't have many options but to advance in the center.} 15.h6 {But we will shove the pawn as far as h6 if allowed! Black's lack of activity and defense has allowed us to challenge their g7-pawn. They could get in a lot of trouble from here, if we continue with moves like Rf1 and transfer the knight to the kingside via c3 and e2. White is much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {They can play the two knights system, developing the knight to f6. It's played 22% of the time. This position can sometimes transpose, but our initial response should stay the same.} 4.Bb5 {Activating the bishop to b5 is the way to go. Black often wants to develop their light-squared bishop which this move restricts as they should defend with ...Bd7. Otherwise, we are threatening to double Black's pawns. 4...Bg4 transposes to the main line.} 4...Bd7 {The most common move is to block the attack with the bishop. It does look passive, but Black shouldn't split their pawn structure.} 5.b3 {We don't have to rush with capturing the knight. Let's just prepare for the bishop to develop and wait for ...a6.} 5...e6 {Black can also choose to play ...e6, which completely blocks their light-squared bishop. In this case, one of the plans is to continue natural development. The other one is aggressive.} 6.Bb2 {We'll get the bishop out to b2.} {The aggressive plan we could go for abstains from castling. Instead, we could try to get an attack going with Qe2 and Rg1 to build up for g4. The additional kingside space with the pawn already on f4 is one of the biggest benefits of the Bird.} 6...Be7 {Black activates their bishop as well, preparing to castle. Let's show a calmer and more of a positional plan next.} 7.O-O {It's to just castle in this position. The king is safe and we can continue development next.} 7...O-O {Black castles as well...} 8.d3 {And we will grab a bit more space with d3. The idea is to often activate the knight to d2 and continue expansion with a move like Qe2. At some point, we could even look to play e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {The most common approach is the more active one, with Black choosing to pin our knight. The idea is to develop the light-squared bishop before playing ...e6. You will face this move 31% of the time. I suggest the active Bb5 here. Instead of defending passively with Be2, we pin Black's knight and threaten to capture it, doubling the black pawns.} 4.Bb5 4...a6 {Black attacking the bishop only helps our cause. We wanted to capture anyway.} 5.Bxc6+ 5...bxc6 {Black recaptures, and here comes the standard plan to develop the dark-squared bishop. We will use this setup in many lines of the Bird. The bishop proves to be very active on the long diagonal. And we keep the flexibility of pushing the d-pawn without having to worry about developing the bishop.} 6.b3 6...Nf6 {Black will play ...Nf6 at some point. And we'll fianchetto.} 7.Bb2 7...e6 {The standard setup for Black as well. These past few moves illustrate the most important approaches for both sides which are seen in a lot of variations in this study. We'll castle, having developed almost all the minor pieces.} 8.O-O 8...Be7 {Black will prepare to castle too. The next piece to develop is the b1-knight. We don't want to activate it to c3 because it doesn't have much potential to jump to an active square from there. d2 is a better square, so we'll prepare that development by playing d3.} 9.d3 9...O-O {Black castles... And we'll bring the knight to d2. From there, the knight controls the key c4 and e4-squares and supports the knight on f3, which is especially helpful when Black plays ...Bg4.} 10.Nbd2 10...c5 {Expect Black to push ...c5 at some point. That's free space to be grabbed and Black wants to undouble their pawns at some point. We don't have to worry about that now, however.} 11.Qe1 {We can simply slide the queen to e1 and unpin. Since we played f4, the queen often gets developed to the kingside from where it targets the light-squared bishop. Also, the g3-square is a good square for the queen if you want to organize an attack, for example.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {The most common approach is the more active one, with Black choosing to pin our knight. The idea is to develop the light-squared bishop before playing ...e6. You will face this move 31% of the time. I suggest the active Bb5 here. Instead of defending passively with Be2, we pin Black's knight and threaten to capture it, doubling the black pawns.} 4.Bb5 4...a6 {Black attacking the bishop only helps our cause. We wanted to capture anyway.} 5.Bxc6+ 5...bxc6 {Black recaptures, and here comes the standard plan to develop the dark-squared bishop. We will use this setup in many lines of the Bird. The bishop proves to be very active on the long diagonal. And we keep the flexibility of pushing the d-pawn without having to worry about developing the bishop.} 6.b3 6...Nf6 {Black will play ...Nf6 at some point. And we'll fianchetto.} 7.Bb2 7...e6 {The standard setup for Black as well. These past few moves illustrate the most important approaches for both sides which are seen in a lot of variations in this study. We'll castle, having developed almost all the minor pieces.} 8.O-O 8...Bxf3 {If Black captures on f3...} 9.Qxf3 {We simply recapture with the queen. The capture on f3 can happen any time, but it's not something likely to cause us problems.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {The most common approach is the more active one, with Black choosing to pin our knight. The idea is to develop the light-squared bishop before playing ...e6. You will face this move 31% of the time. I suggest the active Bb5 here. Instead of defending passively with Be2, we pin Black's knight and threaten to capture it, doubling the black pawns.} 4.Bb5 4...e6 {Black opening up the dark-squared bishop here doesn't change much in our plan. We'll still capture on c6.} 5.Bxc6+ 5...bxc6 {Once Black recaptures, it's time to develop our dark-squared bishop. And the best plan is b3 and Bb2, as per usual.} 6.b3 6...Nf6 {Black will continue natural development... And we will complete the fianchetto.} 7.Bb2 7...Be7 {Black activates the bishop to e7 now, and this is a standard position you might get many times in the Bird. We will simply castle and look to play d3 and Nd2 next.} 8.O-O {Once again, we have a straightforward plan. d3, Nd2 and Qe2 or Qe1 to h4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {The most common approach is the more active one, with Black choosing to pin our knight. The idea is to develop the light-squared bishop before playing ...e6. You will face this move 31% of the time. I suggest the active Bb5 here. Instead of defending passively with Be2, we pin Black's knight and threaten to capture it, doubling the black pawns.} 4.Bb5 4...Qd6 {A move to consider is definitely ...Qd6. We can't damage Black's structure here, so I propose a different plan. It usually isn't advisable to push both d4 and f4 in the Bird, but specific lines require specific countermeasures. One of the ideas is to bring the bishop back to e2 and then solidify the knight on the e5-square. Another one is to push c4 and contest the center, sometimes even pushing c5 if Black castles queenside.} 5.d4 5...a6 {If Black pushes ...a6 here, we won't capture, but rather move according to our plan. And that's to retreat and unpin the knight. Now there's a possibility of Ne5.} 6.Be2 6...Nf6 {Black does have a countermeasure, however. And that is to place the knight on the e4-square, which is the downside of playing d4. We'll castle. There's not much we can do to stop the knight.} 7.O-O 7...e6 {If they continue rather passively, we can just improve.} 8.Nbd2 {We support the knight and prepare Ne5. After that, the c4-advance will be a natural follow-up.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/d5dd8424-1337-4ecd-8f6d-15176ce8489e/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black's most popular first move is 1...d5, and it is played 36% of the time. In the database, this is in more than 17 million games! The logic is: if Black can't place their e-pawn in the center, they can take control of it with the d-pawn. 2.Nf3 is next. We will have two pieces controlling e5. This is a common idea in our opening setups.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Meanwhile, Black is often going to develop in the most natural way when placing a pawn on d5. The knight controls the d4-square. 2...Nc6 is played 24% of the time. The pawn chain we create with e3 is a common theme in the Bird: our main idea is to control the central dark squares. Our dark-squared bishop is blocked for the moment but we have a plan for it: b3 and Bb2! On a side note, it is usually not good to play d4 when we're already played f4. This would leave the e4-square very weak, and our dark-squared bishop would have a hard time developing.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {The most common approach is the more active one, with Black choosing to pin our knight. The idea is to develop the light-squared bishop before playing ...e6. You will face this move 31% of the time. I suggest the active Bb5 here. Instead of defending passively with Be2, we pin Black's knight and threaten to capture it, doubling the black pawns.} 4.Bb5 4...Nf6 {Black can ignore our threat and simply develop. In which case we will capture...} 5.Bxc6+ 5...bxc6 {And worsen their pawn structure. After that, we can continue with our usual plan of developing the dark-squared bishop. Black doesn't really have any threats on our knight.} 6.b3 6...e6 {The best they can do right now is to open up their own bishop. We'll fianchetto ours.} 7.Bb2 7...Be7 {Actually, we encounter this sequence of moves in multiple lines in this chapter. That means it is important to remember the setups like this one. We'll bring the king to safety.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {And so will Black. How to continue now?} 9.d3 {Well, the best plan is to push d3 here and open up a square for the knight. Our knight on b1 will often move to d2 to support the other one. At some point, you can also slide the queen to e1 and look to transfer it to the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...Nf6 or 2...c5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/95b611ed-2112-43bd-abb6-dc88d095ce41/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black places a pawn in the center. We'll develop our knight, pointing at e5.} 2.Nf3 2...c5 {Apart from the Main Line, the move 2...c5 is the most popular move online. It's played 18% of the time, in more than 2 million games! We've given Black space and time to grab more central control, which is exactly why a lot of players play like this. We'll continue as we usually do, creating a dark-square pawn chain. The positions where e3 is a bad move in the Bird are rare.} 3.e3 3...Nc6 {By far the most common third move is ...Nc6. It's played 74% of the time. Black activates the knight behind the pawn and grabs control of the center. We would like to restrict their development. A good move to do that is Bb5, pinning the knight. If Black doesn't protect it, we could capture and ruin their pawn structure.} 4.Bb5 4...Bd7 {That's why ...Bd7 is sensible. We'll just continue as per usual. We'll castle, since we've developed the kingside fully.} 5.O-O 5...a6 {Once Black attacks our bishop... It's okay to capture. Retreating would only give Black more momentum. Besides, we basically placed the bishop on b5 to capture and remove Black's active knight.} 6.Bxc6 6...Bxc6 {Black will recapture with the bishop. Let's continue with the standard developing plan for the dark-squared bishop. We'll fianchetto.} 7.b3 7...Nf6 {Standard moves by both sides in this line. We'll complete the fianchetto. We've got a lot of control of the central dark-squares, especially the e5-square. This means that our knight is coming there.} 8.Bb2 8...e6 {Once Black plays ...e6, we can implement this idea. Now we are attacking the bishop and threatening to disrupt Black's queenside structure again.} 9.Ne5 9...Rc8 {Black defends with ...Rc8. The only minor piece left to activate is the knight on b1. Developing it to c3 would block our bishop and from there the knight wouldn't have many active squares to jump to. That's why I recommend d3 to develop it to d2.} 10.d3 10...Be7 {And once Black gets the bishop out...} 11.Nd2 {We'll do just that. We've got an equal position on our hands. Black will castle and maybe look to play ...b5, which is why a4 could be a good idea. We could capture the bishop on c6 at some point as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...Nf6 or 2...c5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/95b611ed-2112-43bd-abb6-dc88d095ce41/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black places a pawn in the center. We'll develop our knight, pointing at e5.} 2.Nf3 2...c5 {Apart from the Main Line, the move 2...c5 is the most popular move online. It's played 18% of the time, in more than 2 million games! We've given Black space and time to grab more central control, which is exactly why a lot of players play like this. We'll continue as we usually do, creating a dark-square pawn chain. The positions where e3 is a bad move in the Bird are rare.} 3.e3 3...Nc6 {By far the most common third move is ...Nc6. It's played 74% of the time. Black activates the knight behind the pawn and grabs control of the center. We would like to restrict their development. A good move to do that is Bb5, pinning the knight. If Black doesn't protect it, we could capture and ruin their pawn structure.} 4.Bb5 4...Bg4 {Another idea Black could try is ...Bg4, counter-pinning our knight. As mentioned, if Black doesn't protect the c6-knight, we should capture it.} 5.Bxc6+ 5...bxc6 {The pawn recaptures. We don't have to do anything with our knight yet, as Black doesn't have many threats. The usual setup will suffice here, so let's fianchetto.} 6.b3 6...Nf6 {Expect Black to develop naturally as well. The trade-off Black made is pinning our knight for the price of doubling their pawns. We'll fianchetto now. We still want to control those dark squares in the center even if Ne5 is currently impossible.} 7.Bb2 7...e6 {Black advances the e-pawn and opens up the dark-squared bishop. We'll castle and expect Black to do so as well soon.} 8.O-O 8...Be7 {They get the bishop out. As in the main line, developing the knight to c3 might be suboptimal, so we'll aim to get it out to d2.} 9.d3 9...O-O {Black castles.} 10.Nbd2 {And now our f3-knight is supported by another one. White is slightly better here for the simple reason of leading in activity and having a better pawn structure. All in all, a positional but comfortable game for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...Nf6 or 2...c5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/95b611ed-2112-43bd-abb6-dc88d095ce41/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black places a pawn in the center. We'll develop our knight, pointing at e5.} 2.Nf3 2...c5 {Apart from the Main Line, the move 2...c5 is the most popular move online. It's played 18% of the time, in more than 2 million games! We've given Black space and time to grab more central control, which is exactly why a lot of players play like this. We'll continue as we usually do, creating a dark-square pawn chain. The positions where e3 is a bad move in the Bird are rare.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black could activate the knight now, although they only do so 7% of the time. It is a mix of the two main branches of this study. I recommend a specific approach against this. Let's develop with tempo. Be2 is too passive as an alternative. All in all, I think b5 is the best square for our bishop.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Bd7 {Black blocks. But a trade is okay here, as we don't want Black to have an active light-squared bishop.} 5.Bxd7+ 5...Nbxd7 {The knight recaptures, and we can continue with standard development here. The bishop can develop to b2, as per usual.} 6.b3 6...g6 {Setups with ...g6 are annoying because they are harder to attack. But they are rare. We'll develop the bishop to the optimal square...} 7.Bb2 7...Bg7 {And both bishops will find themselves on the long diagonal. This structure for Black is rock solid. We'll castle first.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {But what should our plan be after Black castles?} 9.Qe2 {We'll develop the queen to e2. Our expansion could consist of c4 and Nc3 to contest the center and block the c-file, especially if Black plays ...Rc8.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...Nf6 or 2...c5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/95b611ed-2112-43bd-abb6-dc88d095ce41/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black places a pawn in the center. We'll develop our knight, pointing at e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is played 17% of the time. It sometimes transposes into positions we cover in other lines, but there are some specific setups we need to look at. We will continue naturally with e3.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {Instead of going for ...Nc6 or ...c5, Black can continue with ...Bg4. The idea is that Black wants to get the bishop out before playing ...e6. We should deal with this pin as quickly as possible. If we don't, ...c5 could arrive quickly, so we will attack the bishop right away.} 4.h3 4...Bxf3 {Black captures on f3, as there isn't much merit to retreating now. Of course, ...Bh5 would get the bishop trapped. And we will recapture with the queen.} 5.Qxf3 5...Nc6 {Going for ...Nc6 now changes stuff up a bit. I recommend we pin the knight. The point is that Black has already traded their light-squared bishop so there is no ...Bd7. This means that we will most likely be able to damage their structure.} 6.Bb5 6...e6 {If Black continues with ...e6, just capture that knight. And now Black has to damage their pawns.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {This is a small positional victory for us.} 8.b3 {And now we can simply continue with b3. The bishop is coming to b2, the knight is getting developed and we will castle. There is nothing to worry about in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...Nf6 or 2...c5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/95b611ed-2112-43bd-abb6-dc88d095ce41/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black places a pawn in the center. We'll develop our knight, pointing at e5.} 2.Nf3 2...c5 {Apart from the Main Line, the move 2...c5 is the most popular move online. It's played 18% of the time, in more than 2 million games! We've given Black space and time to grab more central control, which is exactly why a lot of players play like this. We'll continue as we usually do, creating a dark-square pawn chain. The positions where e3 is a bad move in the Bird are rare.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black could activate the knight now, although they only do so 7% of the time. It is a mix of the two main branches of this study. I recommend a specific approach against this. Let's develop with tempo. Be2 is too passive as an alternative. All in all, I think b5 is the best square for our bishop.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Bd7 {Black blocks. But a trade is okay here, as we don't want Black to have an active light-squared bishop.} 5.Bxd7+ 5...Nbxd7 {The knight recaptures, and we can continue with standard development here. The bishop can develop to b2, as per usual.} 6.b3 6...e6 {Since the light-squared bishop has been traded, Black can comfortably play ...e6. Both sides will develop naturally.} 7.Bb2 7...Be7 {And the position is equal. We are about to castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...Nf6 or 2...c5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/95b611ed-2112-43bd-abb6-dc88d095ce41/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black places a pawn in the center. We'll develop our knight, pointing at e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {The move 2...Nf6 is played 17% of the time. It sometimes transposes into positions we cover in other lines, but there are some specific setups we need to look at. We will continue naturally with e3.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {Instead of going for ...Nc6 or ...c5, Black can continue with ...Bg4. The idea is that Black wants to get the bishop out before playing ...e6. We should deal with this pin as quickly as possible. If we don't, ...c5 could arrive quickly, so we will attack the bishop right away.} 4.h3 4...Bxf3 {Black captures on f3, as there isn't much merit to retreating now. Of course, ...Bh5 would get the bishop trapped. And we will recapture with the queen.} 5.Qxf3 5...e6 {Black solidifies the center on the light squares, and the position is equal. Natural development to follow.} 6.b3 {For example, b3, Bb2, and so on.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 3: Black Commits To 2...e6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/7cdcd09b-2d23-4f84-9806-b488d3a42224/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black responds with ...d5. The standard second move, developing the knight behind the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...e6 {Now Black creates a pawn chain in the center. 2...e6 is played 12% of the time, which is still in more than 1.3 million games. This is a solid approach where Black wants to keep their pawns on the light squares and most often try to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. We'll open up our light-squared bishop and plan to fianchetto the other bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {And once Black continues by developing the knight... We'll go for the fianchetto.} 4.b3 4...c5 {The move c5 is the most popular move in this position, played 30% of the time. Black wants to grab space before activating the knight to c6 behind the pawn. This is an active but a risky setup, where Black can easily become vulnerable to kingside attacks. Let's activate the dark-squared bishop first. It's incredibly strong on b2 already.} 5.Bb2 5...Nc6 {Black continues with their plan, and here is an interesting developing move. I suggest activating the bishop to d3! It looks counter-intuitive because it blocks the d-pawn, but the bishop will prove to serve the best purpose on this square. From there, it's eyeing the h7-square and is ready to help the attack as soon as possible.} 6.Bd3 6...Be7 {Black continues in a standard manner, simply developing. This is usually good when you're playing a solid approach, but we've already got some angry bishops out. If Black castles carelessly, we'll go for an attack. First, we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...O-O {And Black does so too. It's time to start improving our pieces and opening up the queen. The knight on f6 is Black's most important defender right now. By centralizing our knight, we offer a trade.} 8.Ne5 8...Nxe5 {Black removes our active knight, but for a price. We will actually recapture with the pawn. The main idea is to kick the knight away from f6.} 9.fxe5 9...Nd7 {Because if it is not on f6... It's not defending the h7-pawn anymore! The position is still equal, but Black has to be extremely precise when defending here.} 10.Qh5 10...g6 {Blocking the threat with the g-pawn could work, but Black has to know what they are doing. We will keep the queen close to the opponent's king. Again, Black can't just make a move they think is good here. There is a specific line where the position remains equal.} 11.Qh6 11...Bg5 {The correct move is ...Bg5. Black improves the bishop and targets our queen. We will escape to h3, the only remaining square.} 12.Qh3 12...Qc7 {Now the queen comes to c7 to attack our e5-pawn. We don't have to protect it just yet, though.} 13.c4 {We can play c4 for now. White is slightly better with an active position. Black shouldn't capture on e5 because of the Qg3 pin.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 3: Black Commits To 2...e6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/7cdcd09b-2d23-4f84-9806-b488d3a42224/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black responds with ...d5. The standard second move, developing the knight behind the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...e6 {Now Black creates a pawn chain in the center. 2...e6 is played 12% of the time, which is still in more than 1.3 million games. This is a solid approach where Black wants to keep their pawns on the light squares and most often try to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. We'll open up our light-squared bishop and plan to fianchetto the other bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {And once Black continues by developing the knight... We'll go for the fianchetto.} 4.b3 4...c5 {The move c5 is the most popular move in this position, played 30% of the time. Black wants to grab space before activating the knight to c6 behind the pawn. This is an active but a risky setup, where Black can easily become vulnerable to kingside attacks. Let's activate the dark-squared bishop first. It's incredibly strong on b2 already.} 5.Bb2 5...Nc6 {Black continues with their plan, and here is an interesting developing move. I suggest activating the bishop to d3! It looks counter-intuitive because it blocks the d-pawn, but the bishop will prove to serve the best purpose on this square. From there, it's eyeing the h7-square and is ready to help the attack as soon as possible.} 6.Bd3 6...Be7 {Black continues in a standard manner, simply developing. This is usually good when you're playing a solid approach, but we've already got some angry bishops out. If Black castles carelessly, we'll go for an attack. First, we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...O-O {And Black does so too. It's time to start improving our pieces and opening up the queen. The knight on f6 is Black's most important defender right now. By centralizing our knight, we offer a trade.} 8.Ne5 8...Nxe5 {Black removes our active knight, but for a price. We will actually recapture with the pawn. The main idea is to kick the knight away from f6.} 9.fxe5 9...Nd7 {Because if it is not on f6... It's not defending the h7-pawn anymore! The position is still equal, but Black has to be extremely precise when defending here.} 10.Qh5 10...g6 {Blocking the threat with the g-pawn could work, but Black has to know what they are doing. We will keep the queen close to the opponent's king. Again, Black can't just make a move they think is good here. There is a specific line where the position remains equal.} 11.Qh6 11...Re8 {For example, sliding the rook to e8 loses on the spot. Black mustn't leave the f7-pawn. Because we will sacrifice the rook immediately!} 12.Rxf7 12...Kxf7 {Black has to capture. This leaves the h7-pawn hanging with check.} 13.Qxh7+ 13...Kf8 {Once the king moves, do you see the finisher?} 14.Bxg6 {It is the simple Bxg6, proving the strength of the light-squared bishop. Now we are threatening two different checkmates in one and Black can't defend against both. Good game.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 3: Black Commits To 2...e6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/7cdcd09b-2d23-4f84-9806-b488d3a42224/lines"]

