ll equal, but Black has to be extremely precise when defending here.} 10.Qh5 10...h6 {For example, the move ...h6 already gives us a huge advantage.} 11.Rf3 {We'll implement a rook lift, ready to slide to g3 and threaten the king even more. If we need to, we will quickly play Na3 and bring the a-rook to the party. White is much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 3: Black Commits To 2...e6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/7cdcd09b-2d23-4f84-9806-b488d3a42224/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black responds with ...d5. The standard second move, developing the knight behind the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...e6 {Now Black creates a pawn chain in the center. 2...e6 is played 12% of the time, which is still in more than 1.3 million games. This is a solid approach where Black wants to keep their pawns on the light squares and most often try to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. We'll open up our light-squared bishop and plan to fianchetto the other bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {And once Black continues by developing the knight... We'll go for the fianchetto.} 4.b3 4...Be7 {You will face 4...Be7 almost as often as 4...c5 here, as it is played 28% of the time. Black just wants a solid setup with active pieces and no real fireworks on the board. But we will still create them. We will fianchetto first.} 5.Bb2 5...O-O {Once Black castles, we will go for the same developing move as in the main line. We don't really need to push the d-pawn, so blocking it is actually not a problem here. Our bishops stand strong.} 6.Bd3 6...b6 {As mentioned, Black will most likely go for a fianchetto approach as well. And this is the moment where we can choose to be creative even without Black pushing ...c5. We can try to force an attack with Rg1! Although it comes with the cost of losing some castling rights, our idea is simple: g4.} 7.Rg1 7...Bb7 {If Black just continues developing, they will have to endure some aggression. And here it comes, with g4!} 8.g4 8...Ne4 {Anticipating g5, many players will jump into the center here. Black does have a clamp on the e4-square after all. We will still push. We don't have an immediate attack, but we managed to grab a lot of space.} 9.g5 9...c5 {When faced with an attack on a side of the board, a general principle is to counter in the center. But here the center is closed, so we have time to pursue our concept further. As the knight on e4 is annoying and it is blocking our bishop, we will start by exchanging it off.} 10.Bxe4 10...dxe4 {Black recaptures... And we centralize our knight on e5. Our plan is to rotate to g4 from where the knight targets the critical dark squares.} 11.Ne5 11...Nd7 {As our knight it good, don't accept this trade if Black offers it.} 12.Ng4 {The knight is now very strong on g4, but our most important piece is the bishop on b2. If we get the queen to the attack, this could get extremely dangerous for Black. For example, if Black plays a random move like ...a6, Nh6+ is already crushing. Black can't capture because of gxh6+, and moving the king to h8 runs into Qh5 in that case.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...g6 Fianchetto / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/ffa06a22-220f-4685-864b-b664f40b1437/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black goes for 1...d5, the most popular response. We'll reinforce our control of the central e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...g6 {And now this move, played in more than 200,000 games (around 2% of the total games), is a very important setup. Black has a dual strategy of the light squares with their d-pawn, and the dark squares with their g7-fianchetto. To start countering, we will be consistent with our plan on the dark squares. We will later use our c-pawn to counter Black's central pawn.} 3.e3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Black now contests the central dark squares more. And we'll attack with the c4-pawn! 4...dxc4 develops our bishop for us.} 4.c4 4...c6 {Black can react naturally and simply defend the pawn in the center. This is in fact the most popular move, happening in more than 2,500 games. We'll capture once. I prefer this to the immediate d4 because it opens up our bishop if we need it.} 5.cxd5 5...cxd5 {Black recaptures with the pawn. And now it's time to contest the center. With d4! Black's bishop is neutralized and we have a huge clamp on the e5-square.} 6.d4 6...Nc6 {Black can activate the knight, but it doesn't have any concrete threats. We can continue developing ourselves. It's up to Black to decide whether they want to protect the center with ...e6 and block the bishop or not.} 7.Nc3 7...Nf6 {For now, they activate the knight and prepare castling. We should place our bishop on the best square, too. And that's d3.} 8.Bd3 8...O-O {Black finally castles... And so will we. Next, we will look to develop the dark-squared bishop to d2, but here it mainly depends on what Black will do.} 9.O-O 9...Bg4 {They decide to activate the bishop first and pin our knight. Sure, this is fine, but the bishop will most likely have to trade. Because when we attack it, there isn't really a good retreating square for Black.} 10.h3 10...Bxf3 {So they capture. We will simply take back with the queen.} 11.Qxf3 11...e6 {Now Black can play on the light squares, but we've got a lot of potential left in our position.} 12.Bd2 {We can play g4 to expand on the kingside, and continue with rook lifts like Rf2 to get better positioned for the attack. We can also choose to play on the queenside with a Rfb1/b4/a4 plan. Ideally, we choose one of the expansions and roll with it.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (1...d5) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...g6 Fianchetto / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/87b2d106-d8f9-415c-841f-7e193baec75b/studies/ffa06a22-220f-4685-864b-b664f40b1437/lines"]

1.f4 1...d5 {Black goes for 1...d5, the most popular response. We'll reinforce our control of the central e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...g6 {And now this move, played in more than 200,000 games (around 2% of the total games), is a very important setup. Black has a dual strategy of the light squares with their d-pawn, and the dark squares with their g7-fianchetto. To start countering, we will be consistent with our plan on the dark squares. We will later use our c-pawn to counter Black's central pawn.} 3.e3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Black now contests the central dark squares more. And we'll attack with the c4-pawn! 4...dxc4 develops our bishop for us.} 4.c4 4...Nf6 {This defense is inferior for Black because it allows us to trade a flank pawn for a central one. We will of course take them up on this offer.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {Black takes on d5. 5...Qxd5 did not make sense because we would get in Nc3. Now our center looks even more powerful after d4. And when there isn't a pawn on d5, you should even aim for e4!} 6.d4 6...O-O {Black castles. Why shouldn't we grab space and attack their knight in the center? All of our pawns on dark squares are defended.} 7.e4 7...Nb6 {The knight retreats to b6 to keep the bishop open and attacking the pawn. Black wants ...Bg4 now to pin our knight. Nevertheless, we'll continue to develop towards the center.} 8.Nc3 8...Bg4 {This pin is not that big of a deal anyway. Because we have a defense and we've calculated what happens if Black is persistent. We'll support the d4-pawn with the bishop.} 9.Be3 9...Bxf3 {And they are. They claim that we have to recapture with the pawn in order to save d4. But our idea is to take back with the queen and offer the pawn! We will receive a lot of compensation for the pawn.} 10.Qxf3 10...Bxd4 {Black captures... And we can pin the bishop here by castling. This forces the bishops off, after which there are weaknesses around the black king and no pieces to defend them.} 11.O-O-O 11...Bxe3+ {Black can capture with check. Naturally we'll recapture on e3. We've managed to get a couple of pieces out in no time and castled. And Black's queen is under attack as well.} 12.Qxe3 12...Qe8 {This means that we can use this momentum to attack on the kingside quickly, while Black's pieces are tangled up and passive. And we will do so with h4! Of course, h5 is the idea.} 13.h4 13...h5 {Which is why Black blocks. However, this is a big mistake. Our attack is nowhere near over.} 14.g4 {The g4-pawn comes in as well! If Black captures, h5 is coming. If we manage to bust open the kingside, our pieces will be able to join the attack rapidly and we should already be winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays ...e5 and ...d6 (Main Line) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/524f10d2-fcb0-42bb-a248-d4c9b059a939/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The idea behind this gambit is to follow it up with either ...d6, trying to develop quickly, or ...Nc6 as we will see in the Sidelines study. Expect to see 1...e5 only 10% of the time, even though that is almost 5 million games. We'll take. After all, this isn't a gambit to reject.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {And now Black offers another pawn. Their idea is simple: for the price of a pawn, they want to have an already developed dark-squared bishop and an open light-squared bishop. I say let them have it. The compensation they get isn't good enough for them.} 3.exd6 3...Bxd6 {The bishop recaptures. But what happens when we just continue natural development with Nf3? We won't be that behind in development at all if the game continues peacefully, and Black won't have much to show for the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...g5 {The move ...g5 is actually the most common move in this position, played 38% of the time! Black is going all out on the early attack. The obvious plan is ...g4, with Qh4+ to follow when the f3-knight moves. To neutralize this, I recommend g3, blocking the potential queen check. It's a good reaction to Black's last move without weakening our king too much.} 5.g3 5...g4 {You will face this push 70% of the time. Black forces the issue, trying to make something out of the position. I suggest we jump to h4. We might need the knight on the kingside and from h4 we can move to g2 if needed.} 6.Nh4 6...Ne7 {One of Black's ideas could be to rotate the knight to g6 and try to trade in order to open up the h-file. This plan does work because our knight on h4 is restricted, but even if the h-file opens up we're fine. We've got time to expand in the center with d4 and open up the dark-squared bishop. We're also going to put the queen on d3. You'll see why.} 7.d4 7...Ng6 {Black goes for the trade. We don't want to retreat to g2 and get tangled up. So we'll capture.} 8.Nxg6 8...hxg6 {The h-pawn recaptures, and the rook is open. Do you see the threat for Black now? The threat was to capture the g3-pawn! That's why Qd3 comes to the rescue, covering the g-pawn.} 9.Qd3 9...Nc6 {Black continues with ...Nc6. We don't want Black complicating matters with ...Nb4 or sacrificing the knight on d4. So the move c3 covers both threats. Still, Black can initiate some aggression in this line.} 10.c3 10...Bf5 {And they definitely will use the chance to develop and attack your queen here. If they know their theory you need to be prepared to defend a bit here. The best defense is e4, blocking the attack and grabbing more central space.} 11.e4 11...Qe7 {They can pin the pawn, and we have to defend it again. But we have a simple way of doing so. And that's to place the bishop on g2. Everything is defended so far.} 12.Bg2 12...O-O-O {Black castles queenside now. The king is safer and their rook can join the game. On the other hand, we will get the bishop to e3. Not only does this reinforce the center, but it also unpins the e4-pawn! We are now threatening to capture the bishop on f5.} 13.Be3 13...Rde8 {A natural move for Black here is ...Rde8, increasing the aggression on the pawn. But it doesn't accomplish much other than preventing us taking the bishop on f5.} 14.Nd2 {We will simply defend e4 and get out of the way on the next move. Black can't add any more attackers to the pawn and White is much better once we castle queenside. We are up a pawn and Black's g-pawns are doubled.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays ...e5 and ...d6 (Main Line) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/524f10d2-fcb0-42bb-a248-d4c9b059a939/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The idea behind this gambit is to follow it up with either ...d6, trying to develop quickly, or ...Nc6 as we will see in the Sidelines study. Expect to see 1...e5 only 10% of the time, even though that is almost 5 million games. We'll take. After all, this isn't a gambit to reject.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {And now Black offers another pawn. Their idea is simple: for the price of a pawn, they want to have an already developed dark-squared bishop and an open light-squared bishop. I say let them have it. The compensation they get isn't good enough for them.} 3.exd6 3...Bxd6 {The bishop recaptures. But what happens when we just continue natural development with Nf3? We won't be that behind in development at all if the game continues peacefully, and Black won't have much to show for the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...g5 {The move ...g5 is actually the most common move in this position, played 38% of the time! Black is going all out on the early attack. The obvious plan is ...g4, with Qh4+ to follow when the f3-knight moves. To neutralize this, I recommend g3, blocking the potential queen check. It's a good reaction to Black's last move without weakening our king too much.} 5.g3 5...g4 {You will face this push 70% of the time. Black forces the issue, trying to make something out of the position. I suggest we jump to h4. We might need the knight on the kingside and from h4 we can move to g2 if needed.} 6.Nh4 6...Nc6 {Black could choose a less confrontational approach here and activate the knight, still claiming an advantage in activity. That gives us time to consolidate, starting with d3. Here we can implement a standard developing setup with the knight on c3 and the bishop on g2. Don't make a mistake and play b3 here because there is ...Be5 and the position suddenly gets complicated!} 7.d3 7...Be6 {Black continues by getting the bishop out. They will castle long since the kingside is weakened. They have a lead in development now, but that won't prove to be decisive. Especially when we get a knight that controls the center.} 8.Nc3 8...Qd7 {And, once Black prepares to castle long... We also get a monster bishop on g2, eyeing that queenside. We've consolidated and can only go up from here.} 9.Bg2 9...O-O-O {Black castles.} 10.O-O {And so do we. Now we've also got the semi-open f-file for our rook. Again, Black has nothing to show for the pawn and White is much better, with prospects of e4 or Ne4. Black's own g-pawn is blocking the bishop and the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays ...e5 and ...d6 (Main Line) / Variation 2"]
