1.f4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The idea behind this gambit is to follow it up with either ...d6, trying to develop quickly, or ...Nc6 as we will see in the Sidelines study. Expect to see 1...e5 only 10% of the time, even though that is almost 5 million games. We'll take. After all, this isn't a gambit to reject.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {And now Black offers another pawn. Their idea is simple: for the price of a pawn, they want to have an already developed dark-squared bishop and an open light-squared bishop. I say let them have it. The compensation they get isn't good enough for them.} 3.exd6 3...Bxd6 {The bishop recaptures. But what happens when we just continue natural development with Nf3? We won't be that behind in development at all if the game continues peacefully, and Black won't have much to show for the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...g5 {The move ...g5 is actually the most common move in this position, played 38% of the time! Black is going all out on the early attack. The obvious plan is ...g4, with Qh4+ to follow when the f3-knight moves. To neutralize this, I recommend g3, blocking the potential queen check. It's a good reaction to Black's last move without weakening our king too much.} 5.g3 5...g4 {You will face this push 70% of the time. Black forces the issue, trying to make something out of the position. I suggest we jump to h4. We might need the knight on the kingside and from h4 we can move to g2 if needed.} 6.Nh4 6...Ne7 {One of Black's ideas could be to rotate the knight to g6 and try to trade in order to open up the h-file. This plan does work because our knight on h4 is restricted, but even if the h-file opens up we're fine. We've got time to expand in the center with d4 and open up the dark-squared bishop. We're also going to put the queen on d3. You'll see why.} 7.d4 7...Ng6 {Black goes for the trade. We don't want to retreat to g2 and get tangled up. So we'll capture.} 8.Nxg6 8...hxg6 {The h-pawn recaptures, and the rook is open. Do you see the threat for Black now? The threat was to capture the g3-pawn! That's why Qd3 comes to the rescue, covering the g-pawn.} 9.Qd3 9...Nc6 {Black continues with ...Nc6. We don't want Black complicating matters with ...Nb4 or sacrificing the knight on d4. So the move c3 covers both threats. Still, Black can initiate some aggression in this line.} 10.c3 10...Bf5 {And they definitely will use the chance to develop and attack your queen here. If they know their theory you need to be prepared to defend a bit here. The best defense is e4, blocking the attack and grabbing more central space.} 11.e4 11...Qe7 {They can pin the pawn, and we have to defend it again. But we have a simple way of doing so. And that's to place the bishop on g2. Everything is defended so far.} 12.Bg2 12...O-O-O {Black castles queenside now. The king is safer and their rook can join the game. On the other hand, we will get the bishop to e3. Not only does this reinforce the center, but it also unpins the e4-pawn! We are now threatening to capture the bishop on f5.} 13.Be3 13...Rxh2 {Black might think they have a tactic here with ...Rxh2. We'll take the rook.} 14.Rxh2 14...Bxg3+ {The point is to win back the rook with this fork. But we'll just move the king out of check.} 15.Kd1 15...Bxh2 {Once they capture our rook...} 16.exf5 {We can take their bishop! We are up a piece and completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays ...e5 and ...d6 (Main Line) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/524f10d2-fcb0-42bb-a248-d4c9b059a939/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The idea behind this gambit is to follow it up with either ...d6, trying to develop quickly, or ...Nc6 as we will see in the Sidelines study. Expect to see 1...e5 only 10% of the time, even though that is almost 5 million games. We'll take. After all, this isn't a gambit to reject.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {And now Black offers another pawn. Their idea is simple: for the price of a pawn, they want to have an already developed dark-squared bishop and an open light-squared bishop. I say let them have it. The compensation they get isn't good enough for them.} 3.exd6 3...Bxd6 {The bishop recaptures. But what happens when we just continue natural development with Nf3? We won't be that behind in development at all if the game continues peacefully, and Black won't have much to show for the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...g5 {The move ...g5 is actually the most common move in this position, played 38% of the time! Black is going all out on the early attack. The obvious plan is ...g4, with Qh4+ to follow when the f3-knight moves. To neutralize this, I recommend g3, blocking the potential queen check. It's a good reaction to Black's last move without weakening our king too much.} 5.g3 5...g4 {You will face this push 70% of the time. Black forces the issue, trying to make something out of the position. I suggest we jump to h4. We might need the knight on the kingside and from h4 we can move to g2 if needed.} 6.Nh4 6...Ne7 {One of Black's ideas could be to rotate the knight to g6 and try to trade in order to open up the h-file. This plan does work because our knight on h4 is restricted, but even if the h-file opens up we're fine. We've got time to expand in the center with d4 and open up the dark-squared bishop. We're also going to put the queen on d3. You'll see why.} 7.d4 7...Ng6 {Black goes for the trade. We don't want to retreat to g2 and get tangled up. So we'll capture.} 8.Nxg6 8...hxg6 {The h-pawn recaptures, and the rook is open. Do you see the threat for Black now? The threat was to capture the g3-pawn! That's why Qd3 comes to the rescue, covering the g-pawn.} 9.Qd3 9...Nc6 {Black continues with ...Nc6. We don't want Black complicating matters with ...Nb4 or sacrificing the knight on d4. So the move c3 covers both threats. Still, Black can initiate some aggression in this line.} 10.c3 10...Bf5 {And they definitely will use the chance to develop and attack your queen here. If they know their theory you need to be prepared to defend a bit here. The best defense is e4, blocking the attack and grabbing more central space.} 11.e4 11...Qe7 {They can pin the pawn, and we have to defend it again. But we have a simple way of doing so. And that's to place the bishop on g2. Everything is defended so far.} 12.Bg2 12...O-O-O {Black castles queenside now. The king is safer and their rook can join the game. On the other hand, we will get the bishop to e3. Not only does this reinforce the center, but it also unpins the e4-pawn! We are now threatening to capture the bishop on f5.} 13.Be3 13...Bd7 {The best move according to the computer is to simply retreat with the bishop. Black keeps some chances here although White is still better. The threat is now ...Rxh2. We'll move away from the tactic by playing Kd1.} 14.Kd1 14...f5 {Sure, Black can still try to strike in the center...} 15.Nd2 {But we don't have to capture and expose the e-file. We can just strengthen the center instead and look to get the king to a safer square next and connect the rooks. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays ...e5 and ...d6 (Main Line) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/524f10d2-fcb0-42bb-a248-d4c9b059a939/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The idea behind this gambit is to follow it up with either ...d6, trying to develop quickly, or ...Nc6 as we will see in the Sidelines study. Expect to see 1...e5 only 10% of the time, even though that is almost 5 million games. We'll take. After all, this isn't a gambit to reject.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {And now Black offers another pawn. Their idea is simple: for the price of a pawn, they want to have an already developed dark-squared bishop and an open light-squared bishop. I say let them have it. The compensation they get isn't good enough for them.} 3.exd6 3...Bxd6 {The bishop recaptures. But what happens when we just continue natural development with Nf3? We won't be that behind in development at all if the game continues peacefully, and Black won't have much to show for the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...g5 {The move ...g5 is actually the most common move in this position, played 38% of the time! Black is going all out on the early attack. The obvious plan is ...g4, with Qh4+ to follow when the f3-knight moves. To neutralize this, I recommend g3, blocking the potential queen check. It's a good reaction to Black's last move without weakening our king too much.} 5.g3 5...h5 {Black doesn't have to play ...g4 first. Around 20% of the time, players choose to follow up the g-pawn with the h-pawn. This is obviously over-extending, but those pawns do look dangerous. However, one of the best antidotes to the attack on a side of the board is playing in the center. We'll expand, grabbing space and targeting the g-pawn.} 6.d4 6...g4 {The g5-pawn will most likely get pushed. The knight is on the edge of the board but safe, and even restricting Black's light-squared bishop for now.} 7.Nh4 7...Be7 {If Black lines up the bishop with the queen, claiming we don't have a lot of squares for the knight... We can actually give the pawn back. In the upcoming line, we will get a lot of activity and be clearly better. One of the reasons we played d4 is so that we can play Bf4 here.} 8.Bf4 8...Bxh4 {Black captures. We'll take back.} 9.gxh4 9...Qxh4+ {The queen comes in with check, but we played Bf4 for a reason. And that's to block this check and grab a tempo.} 10.Bg3 10...Qe7 {Black has to retreat.} 11.Qd2 {And now we simply bring the queen up one square. Once we get the knight out, we will be ready to castle queenside. Our bishop on g3 is already extremely strong and will be joined by the other bishop on g2. Meanwhile, Black hasn't achieved anything on the kingside other than over-extending. They are passive and their king is weak. If Black goes for ...h4, don't be afraid to slide right back to f4. The evaluation of this position is +2 for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays ...e5 and ...d6 (Main Line) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/524f10d2-fcb0-42bb-a248-d4c9b059a939/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The idea behind this gambit is to follow it up with either ...d6, trying to develop quickly, or ...Nc6 as we will see in the Sidelines study. Expect to see 1...e5 only 10% of the time, even though that is almost 5 million games. We'll take. After all, this isn't a gambit to reject.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {And now Black offers another pawn. Their idea is simple: for the price of a pawn, they want to have an already developed dark-squared bishop and an open light-squared bishop. I say let them have it. The compensation they get isn't good enough for them.} 3.exd6 3...Bxd6 {The bishop recaptures. But what happens when we just continue natural development with Nf3? We won't be that behind in development at all if the game continues peacefully, and Black won't have much to show for the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...g5 {The move ...g5 is actually the most common move in this position, played 38% of the time! Black is going all out on the early attack. The obvious plan is ...g4, with Qh4+ to follow when the f3-knight moves. To neutralize this, I recommend g3, blocking the potential queen check. It's a good reaction to Black's last move without weakening our king too much.} 5.g3 5...g4 {You will face this push 70% of the time. Black forces the issue, trying to make something out of the position. I suggest we jump to h4. We might need the knight on the kingside and from h4 we can move to g2 if needed.} 6.Nh4 6...Be7 {We could face ...Be7 immediately. Black does move the bishop twice in the opening, but attacks our vulnerable knight. We don't have time for Bf4. So we will retreat to g2. From there, the knight can jump to f4 and free up the g2-square for the bishop.} 7.Ng2 7...h5 {Especially when Black plays ...h5, with the intention of ...h4. Now the knight is more active and still controlling some key squares. Here, we have to be ready for ...h4. Our goal is still to develop, but remain conscious of what Black is doing.} 8.Nf4 8...h4 {And they will push that pawn. Of course, we can't let them capture now that our h-pawn is pinned to the rook. So we will play Bg2 and protect the rook while activating the bishop.} 9.Bg2 9...hxg3 {Black can trade now, but that doesn't bother us anymore. We'll simply recapture, and now the trade of rooks is basically inevitable.} 10.hxg3 10...Rxh1+ {Black captures.} 11.Bxh1 {And we take back. The only thing left to do to prove our advantage is expand in the center, develop the queenside and then castle. Black doesn't have anything here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays ...e5 and ...d6 (Main Line) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/524f10d2-fcb0-42bb-a248-d4c9b059a939/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The idea behind this gambit is to follow it up with either ...d6, trying to develop quickly, or ...Nc6 as we will see in the Sidelines study. Expect to see 1...e5 only 10% of the time, even though that is almost 5 million games. We'll take. After all, this isn't a gambit to reject.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {And now Black offers another pawn. Their idea is simple: for the price of a pawn, they want to have an already developed dark-squared bishop and an open light-squared bishop. I say let them have it. The compensation they get isn't good enough for them.} 3.exd6 3...Bxd6 {The bishop recaptures. But what happens when we just continue natural development with Nf3? We won't be that behind in development at all if the game continues peacefully, and Black won't have much to show for the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...g5 {The move ...g5 is actually the most common move in this position, played 38% of the time! Black is going all out on the early attack. The obvious plan is ...g4, with Qh4+ to follow when the f3-knight moves. To neutralize this, I recommend g3, blocking the potential queen check. It's a good reaction to Black's last move without weakening our king too much.} 5.g3 5...g4 {You will face this push 70% of the time. Black forces the issue, trying to make something out of the position. I suggest we jump to h4. We might need the knight on the kingside and from h4 we can move to g2 if needed.} 6.Nh4 6...Be7 {We could face ...Be7 immediately. Black does move the bishop twice in the opening, but attacks our vulnerable knight. We don't have time for Bf4. So we will retreat to g2. From there, the knight can jump to f4 and free up the g2-square for the bishop.} 7.Ng2 7...h5 {Especially when Black plays ...h5, with the intention of ...h4. Now the knight is more active and still controlling some key squares. Here, we have to be ready for ...h4. Our goal is still to develop, but remain conscious of what Black is doing.} 8.Nf4 8...h4 {And they will push that pawn. Of course, we can't let them capture now that our h-pawn is pinned to the rook. So we will play Bg2 and protect the rook while activating the bishop.} 9.Bg2 9...h3 {What if Black pushes ...h3? Well, they've just blocked all their fronts of attack on the kingside for a one-move threat on the bishop. Although they do get some space. We'll just move to the center.} 10.Be4 10...Nf6 {Black can attack the bishop again, but we won't let them win more tempi.} 11.Nc3 {We'll develop and protect the bishop. This trade is fine for us because despite potentially losing the bishop on the long diagonal, we will have two active knights and Black's lead in activity will diminish. Remember that we are still up a pawn and Black has no attack. We'll castle kingside and maintain advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 2: 1...e5 Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/f1a7cf7d-959b-4348-a91b-1e44c52d23ab/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black gambits a pawn. Let's accept.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {Black plays d6, as before. Let's take the pawn as we did in the Main Lines study.} 3.exd6 3...Qxd6 {Black can also recapture with the queen here. This looks counter intuitive and is played only 3% of the time. However, we have to be prepared for anything. For now, simple development with Nf3 will suffice.} 4.Nf3 4...Bg4 {Black might activate the bishop here, as the other one is blocked. e3 is logical to open up the light-squared bishop and defend the knight with our queen.} 5.e3 5...Nc6 {Black activates the knight to c6 now. The whole point of this line is that they get to castle queenside as quickly as possible. Also, we don't really want the knight coming to e5. So we will unpin. This looks passive, but remember we are up a pawn and Black has done nothing to actually attack our position.} 6.Be2 6...O-O-O {They castle...} 7.O-O {And so will we, safe and sound. With little to no compensation for the pawn, White is simply better. If Black continues to be aggressive with a move like ...f5, even d4 followed by c4 and Nd2 to trade the bishops is a good idea.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 2: 1...e5 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/f1a7cf7d-959b-4348-a91b-1e44c52d23ab/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black gambits a pawn. Let's accept.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {Black plays d6, as before. Let's take the pawn as we did in the Main Lines study.} 3.exd6 3...Bxd6 {Black will most likely recapture with the bishop. And we will continue development, just like in the Main Lines study. We have already analyzed Black's aggressive 4...g5, aiming for ...g4. But what if Black decides to play more of a positional game and develop rapidly, trying to claim an advantage that way?} 4.Nf3 4...Nf6 {A move that definitely signifies rapid development is 4...Nf6. Expect to face this move 20% of the time. I recommend a fianchetto approach here. Black has remained flexible with their last move and they can still try to throw a surprise attack. This way, we ensure to develop the bishop and castle quickly.} 5.g3 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll fianchetto the bishop.} 6.Bg2 6...Nc6 {Not much to do for Black but continue natural development. We've developed the kingside fully... So it's time to castle.} 7.O-O 7...Bg4 {If you see the bishop on g4 you don't have to move the e-pawn. The knight is already defended by the bishop and the rook. Let's devise a plan on how to continue development.} 8.d4 {One such idea could definitely be d4, expanding in the center and opening up the bishop. Next we'll have to choose between c3 and c4, as well as Bg5. White is better but has to be careful.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 2: 1...e5 Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/f1a7cf7d-959b-4348-a91b-1e44c52d23ab/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black gambits a pawn. Let's accept.} 2.fxe5 2...Nc6 {The move 2...Nc6 is played 22% of the time, in more than 600 000 games. The idea is that Black develops quickly, and the e5-pawn can't be saved either way. Let's activate our knight to c3. This either forces Black to capture on e5, or play ...d6 and transpose back into lines we know.} 3.Nc3 3...Nxe5 {Black captures. Sure, they've regained the pawn, but at what cost? We now get to expand in the center with tempo. And guess what, the e-pawn is coming to e4 as well!} 4.d4 4...Nc6 {Black retreating to c6 doesn't accomplish anything. We'll simply play e4, after which we've got two pawns in the center and a huge lead in activity.} 5.e4 5...Bb4 {Black can try to pin our knight...} 6.Nf3 {But we should just develop. If they capture, they will only trade off an active piece. White is much better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 2: 1...e5 Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/f1a7cf7d-959b-4348-a91b-1e44c52d23ab/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black gambits a pawn. Let's accept.} 2.fxe5 2...Nc6 {The move 2...Nc6 is played 22% of the time, in more than 600 000 games. The idea is that Black develops quickly, and the e5-pawn can't be saved either way. Let's activate our knight to c3. This either forces Black to capture on e5, or play ...d6 and transpose back into lines we know.} 3.Nc3 3...Nxe5 {Black captures. Sure, they've regained the pawn, but at what cost? We now get to expand in the center with tempo. And guess what, the e-pawn is coming to e4 as well!} 4.d4 4...Ng6 {Black can try to rotate the knight to the kingside, but this shouldn't change our plan. And it is very simple, which is the beauty of it. We simply want two pawns in the center. After that, we'll develop the knight to f3 and the light-squared bishop - easy moves that play themselves.} 5.e4 5...Bb4 {Black pins our queenside knight... But that doesn't bother us. They can capture if they like. We'll simply develop.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {The knight comes out to f6, and now there is an actual threat on the e4-pawn.} 7.Bd3 {So we protect it. Everything is safe and sound, and we have gotten what every player wishes out of the opening. The kingside is developed and we'll castle next.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 2: 1...e5 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/f1a7cf7d-959b-4348-a91b-1e44c52d23ab/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black gambits a pawn. Let's accept.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {Black plays d6, as before. Let's take the pawn as we did in the Main Lines study.} 3.exd6 3...Bxd6 {Black will most likely recapture with the bishop. And we will continue development, just like in the Main Lines study. We have already analyzed Black's aggressive 4...g5, aiming for ...g4. But what if Black decides to play more of a positional game and develop rapidly, trying to claim an advantage that way?} 4.Nf3 4...Bg4 {One way do try that is ...Bg4. Developing both bishops already, Black is attacking our knight and posing some questions. Their idea is to play ...Nf6 or ...Nc6 next. The move e3 is often a good idea when Black attacks your knight like this. Although it pins the knight, it protects it with the queen and we remain flexible.} 5.e3 5...Nc6 {5...Nc6 is more committal than 5...Nf6. The reason is that we have a clear plan for the light-squared bishop now. We'll develop it to b5 and pin the knight on c6. We've seen this motif in the previous chapter as well. Our idea is to capture!} 6.Bb5 6...a6 {Black will only accelerate that process if they attack our bishop now. We'll take and get rid of the potentially dangerous knight.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {Black's pawns are now doubled and isolated. We'll castle. Next, our plan is simple: we want to ask the bishop on g4 a question with h3, then develop the queenside and play in the center.} 8.O-O 8...Nf6 {Black continues to develop. We'll attack the bishop first.} 9.h3 9...Bh5 {Black can retreat and keep the pin. Chasing the bishop here could be too weakening for the king. So we'll just get the knight out to c3 instead. You will see our plan soon.} 10.Nc3 10...O-O {Black castles first...} 11.d3 {And the idea of d3 is to support the e-pawn advancing. If we can put e4 into motion, we will also get Bg5 out pretty quickly and already start to threaten nasty things in the center. If somehow not, going for b3 and Bb2 is still a sound plan. A move like Kh1 could be a prophylactic idea to get off the dark diagonal.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Combative 1...e5 / Study 2: 1...e5 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/c7c64750-503d-482c-a600-1555c090e51e/studies/f1a7cf7d-959b-4348-a91b-1e44c52d23ab/lines"]

1.f4 1...e5 {Black gambits a pawn. Let's accept.} 2.fxe5 2...d6 {Black plays d6, as before. Let's take the pawn as we did in the Main Lines study.} 3.exd6 3...Bxd6 {Black will most likely recapture with the bishop. And we will continue development, just like in the Main Lines study. We have already analyzed Black's aggressive 4...g5, aiming for ...g4. But what if Black decides to play more of a positional game and develop rapidly, trying to claim an advantage that way?} 4.Nf3 4...Bg4 {One way do try that is ...Bg4. Developing both bishops already, Black is attacking our knight and posing some questions. Their idea is to play ...Nf6 or ...Nc6 next. The move e3 is often a good idea when Black attacks your knight like this. Although it pins the knight, it protects it with the queen and we remain flexible.} 5.e3 5...Nf6 {If Black goes for ...Nf6, they remain open with their options so we have to approach the position a little bit more carefully. I suggest we simply develop with Nc3. Nothing can go wrong here.} 6.Nc3 6...O-O {Black castles. The best square for our bishop now is actually Be2, since Bb5 makes no sense. It does seem cramped, but it's safe.} 7.Be2 7...Nc6 {Black gets their knight out now...} 8.O-O {And we'll castle. We are up a pawn with a solid position, ready to continue expanding. White is much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's c5 Setups / Study 1: Black Plays ...c5 and ...Nc6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/413e0f8d-457b-4bd8-971d-7f007239df21/studies/59364afa-968b-43d9-b113-8f5772cf2481/lines"]

1.f4 1...c5 {Black controls the d4-square using the flank pawn. This move occurs about 11% of the time in more than 5 million games. Our plan stays consistent. We want to control the central e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {This is Black's most popular second move when they start with 1...c5. You can expect it close to 50% of times. Once the c-pawn is pushed, players like to develop this knight and have it fight for central control. We will now build our powerful pawn chain and open up our light-squared bishop. Our ideas for many of these lines will include Bb5 to take on c6 and reduce Black's central grip.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {This move is played less than 10% of times, but I still feel it's natural and worth looking at. It's developing both knights before bishops, after all. Our bishop to b5 idea works here, too. Our aim is to damage the pawns and remove one of Black's best pieces that challenges our dark-square complex in the center.} 4.Bb5 4...e6 {This move ignores our intention to take on c6 and seeks to push d5. We'll take and double the pawns.} 5.Bxc6 5...bxc6 {Black recapturing towards the center is best because they keep the option alive of playing ...d5. We should therefore put our c4-pawn up and claim some central control.} 6.c4 6...d5 {Black now pushes 6...d5. We will leave the pawn tension as it is and focus on development. With b3, we support c4 and get ready to fianchetto.} 7.b3 7...Bd6 {Black develops their bishop to d6, fighting for e5. But after Bb2 we have two pieces and a pawn controlling e5.} 8.Bb2 8...O-O {Black castles. And we'll castle as well.} 9.O-O 9...Ba6 {Black is able to get some pressure against our queenside in this variation. But it's nothing we won't be able to handle with some clean, logical moves. d3 supports our c-pawn.} 10.d3 10...Rb8 {The rook goes to b8 and pins the b-pawn.} 11.Nbd2 {But we can add a defender to c4. We will continue with moves like Qe2 to defend e3 and Ne5. A rook lift can also be possible under the right circumstances.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's c5 Setups / Study 1: Black Plays ...c5 and ...Nc6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/413e0f8d-457b-4bd8-971d-7f007239df21/studies/59364afa-968b-43d9-b113-8f5772cf2481/lines"]

1.f4 1...c5 {Black controls the d4-square using the flank pawn. This move occurs about 11% of the time in more than 5 million games. Our plan stays consistent. We want to control the central e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {This is Black's most popular second move when they start with 1...c5. You can expect it close to 50% of times. Once the c-pawn is pushed, players like to develop this knight and have it fight for central control. We will now build our powerful pawn chain and open up our light-squared bishop. Our ideas for many of these lines will include Bb5 to take on c6 and reduce Black's central grip.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {This move is played less than 10% of times, but I still feel it's natural and worth looking at. It's developing both knights before bishops, after all. Our bishop to b5 idea works here, too. Our aim is to damage the pawns and remove one of Black's best pieces that challenges our dark-square complex in the center.} 4.Bb5 4...Qc7 {Black may try avoiding the doubling of the pawns by bringing their queen out. It's a clever move that's not often seen. We'll just castle for now and wait and see if Black will prompt us to take.} 5.O-O 5...a6 {Once they waste time begging for this trade, we'll accept it. This trade gives us even more power over the e5-square.} 6.Bxc6 6...Qxc6 {The queen recaptures. We're going to fianchetto, which further strengthens our control of e5.} 7.b3 7...d6 {Black pushes ...d6 to fight for control of e5.} 8.Bb2 {We will play Qe2, d3 Nbd2 and e4. Our plans are clear: we will use our central pawn and piece play to attack Black. It is tough to suggest a productive plan for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's c5 Setups / Study 1: Black Plays ...c5 and ...Nc6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/413e0f8d-457b-4bd8-971d-7f007239df21/studies/59364afa-968b-43d9-b113-8f5772cf2481/lines"]

1.f4 1...c5 {Black controls the d4-square using the flank pawn. This move occurs about 11% of the time in more than 5 million games. Our plan stays consistent. We want to control the central e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {This is Black's most popular second move when they start with 1...c5. You can expect it close to 50% of times. Once the c-pawn is pushed, players like to develop this knight and have it fight for central control. We will now build our powerful pawn chain and open up our light-squared bishop. Our ideas for many of these lines will include Bb5 to take on c6 and reduce Black's central grip.} 3.e3 3...g6 {About 20% of games go like this. This move order stops us playing b3 and Bb2. We control the b4-square. This avoids ...Nb4 attacking our bishop.} 4.Bb5 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Their hope is to have their dark-squared bishop actively pressure us on the diagonal. We will take on c6.} 5.Bxc6 5...bxc6 {Taking towards the center is the best move. There's no reason to take away from the center and leave us with even more central control. Now d3 serves multiple purposes. We can play e4 if needed and we also control some central squares.} 6.d3 6...d5 {Black can push in the center right away. We'll castle. They seem to have a strong block of pawns controlling many squares.} 7.O-O 7...Nf6 {They develop their knight to castle kingside. But we also develop our knight. There isn't much we can do about the block of pawns right now, but we are preparing e4.} 8.Nc3 8...O-O {Black castles. We'll put e4 on the board. We have some attacking ideas like e5 and Qe1 to get the queen to a more active square like h4.} 9.e4 9...d4 {Black pushes and attacks us. This is a common reaction, to advance the pawn. The good thing is that now the block of pawns is frozen and we can start building some pressure against them. We'll start with Na4.} 10.Na4 10...Qa5 {Black defends and attacks us.} 11.b3 {But Black is in some trouble because they mismanaged their pawn play. Our very straightforward plans can get you to a position as favorable as this, because Black often plays planlessly. Ba3 and Ne5 are two powerful moves that you will be able to play.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's c5 Setups / Study 1: Black Plays ...c5 and ...Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/413e0f8d-457b-4bd8-971d-7f007239df21/studies/59364afa-968b-43d9-b113-8f5772cf2481/lines"]

1.f4 1...c5 {Black controls the d4-square using the flank pawn. This move occurs about 11% of the time in more than 5 million games. Our plan stays consistent. We want to control the central e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {This is Black's most popular second move when they start with 1...c5. You can expect it close to 50% of times. Once the c-pawn is pushed, players like to develop this knight and have it fight for central control. We will now build our powerful pawn chain and open up our light-squared bishop. Our ideas for many of these lines will include Bb5 to take on c6 and reduce Black's central grip.} 3.e3 3...g6 {About 20% of games go like this. This move order stops us playing b3 and Bb2. We control the b4-square. This avoids ...Nb4 attacking our bishop.} 4.Bb5 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Their hope is to have their dark-squared bishop actively pressure us on the diagonal. We will take on c6.} 5.Bxc6 5...bxc6 {Taking towards the center is the best move. There's no reason to take away from the center and leave us with even more central control. Now d3 serves multiple purposes. We can play e4 if needed and we also control some central squares.} 6.d3 6...Nf6 {Black could delay playing in the center like this. They may simply want to develop. We'll play our slow pawn advance with e4.} 7.e4 7...O-O {They castle. If 8.e5 they play 8...Nd5, so we don't want that. Instead we'll just castle.} 8.O-O 8...d6 {Black gets ready to develop their light-squared bishop.} 9.Nc3 {And we have some great attacking ideas: Be3, Qe1 to h4 and Rb1 to defend b2 if necessary. It's hard to suggest plans for Black because 9...d5 10.e5 is bad for them.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's c5 Setups / Study 1: Black Plays ...c5 and ...Nc6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/413e0f8d-457b-4bd8-971d-7f007239df21/studies/59364afa-968b-43d9-b113-8f5772cf2481/lines"]

1.f4 1...c5 {Black controls the d4-square using the flank pawn. This move occurs about 11% of the time in more than 5 million games. Our plan stays consistent. We want to control the central e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {This is Black's most popular second move when they start with 1...c5. You can expect it close to 50% of times. Once the c-pawn is pushed, players like to develop this knight and have it fight for central control. We will now build our powerful pawn chain and open up our light-squared bishop. Our ideas for many of these lines will include Bb5 to take on c6 and reduce Black's central grip.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {This move is played less than 10% of times, but I still feel it's natural and worth looking at. It's developing both knights before bishops, after all. Our bishop to b5 idea works here, too. Our aim is to damage the pawns and remove one of Black's best pieces that challenges our dark-square complex in the center.} 4.Bb5 4...e6 {This move ignores our intention to take on c6 and seeks to push d5. We'll take and double the pawns.} 5.Bxc6 5...dxc6 {Black recaptures away from the center. This move is inferior because it gives us e5 to work with. d6 cannot be played anymore. We'll fianchetto and focus on increasing our control of e5.} 6.b3 6...Be7 {The bishop is developed. We'll fianchetto, with long range pressure on the diagonal.} 7.Bb2 7...O-O {Black castles. We'll do likewise.} 8.O-O 8...b6 {This is one of the only ways to develop the bishop into an active role. We push d3, again with our slow plan of a potential e4, Nbd2.} 9.d3 9...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the other bishop.} 10.e4 {Black is in serious trouble because the doubled c-pawns don't contribute much and rather block the bishops. We will play Nbd2 and Qe2 and build on our position from there.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's c5 Setups / Study 1: Black Plays ...c5 and ...Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/413e0f8d-457b-4bd8-971d-7f007239df21/studies/59364afa-968b-43d9-b113-8f5772cf2481/lines"]

1.f4 1...c5 {Black controls the d4-square using the flank pawn. This move occurs about 11% of the time in more than 5 million games. Our plan stays consistent. We want to control the central e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {This is Black's most popular second move when they start with 1...c5. You can expect it close to 50% of times. Once the c-pawn is pushed, players like to develop this knight and have it fight for central control. We will now build our powerful pawn chain and open up our light-squared bishop. Our ideas for many of these lines will include Bb5 to take on c6 and reduce Black's central grip.} 3.e3 3...d6 {The most popular move here, 3...d5, transposes back to Chapter 1, Study 2. This alternative is played in about 22% of games. Black defends c5 and gets ready to develop their light-squared bishop. We'll push 4.d4 and create tension in the center.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {You'll most likely see this capture because Black wants to avoid us capturing on c5 ourselves. We'll recapture, with a strong central pawn on d4. Our 6.d5 idea is hard to stop. In fact, 5...g6 and 5...Bg4 can also be met with 6.d5!} 5.exd4 5...e6 {This move prevents us from pushing d5 and attacking the knight on c6. There are about 5,000 games like this on the database (16% of the total), so it isn't unreasonable that someone could play it against you. We will simply develop our light-squared bishop.} 6.Bd3 6...Nf6 {Black brings out the other knight. As will we. We have two powerful central pawns backed up by our pieces.} 7.Nc3 7...Be7 {The bishop comes out to e7. We'll take control of the b4-square. This prevents ...Nb4 attacking our bishop.} 8.a3 8...O-O {Black castles.} 9.Qe2 {We develop the queen, and we have a rich game with various options. We can play Bd2 intending 0-0-0, followed by a kingside attack. Alternatively, we can castle short and place our rooks on e1/f1, then slowly improve with Ne4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's c5 Setups / Study 1: Black Plays ...c5 and ...Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/413e0f8d-457b-4bd8-971d-7f007239df21/studies/59364afa-968b-43d9-b113-8f5772cf2481/lines"]

1.f4 1...c5 {Black controls the d4-square using the flank pawn. This move occurs about 11% of the time in more than 5 million games. Our plan stays consistent. We want to control the central e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {This is Black's most popular second move when they start with 1...c5. You can expect it close to 50% of times. Once the c-pawn is pushed, players like to develop this knight and have it fight for central control. We will now build our powerful pawn chain and open up our light-squared bishop. Our ideas for many of these lines will include Bb5 to take on c6 and reduce Black's central grip.} 3.e3 3...d6 {The most popular move here, 3...d5, transposes back to Chapter 1, Study 2. This alternative is played in about 22% of games. Black defends c5 and gets ready to develop their light-squared bishop. We'll push 4.d4 and create tension in the center.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {You'll most likely see this capture because Black wants to avoid us capturing on c5 ourselves. We'll recapture, with a strong central pawn on d4. Our 6.d5 idea is hard to stop. In fact, 5...g6 and 5...Bg4 can also be met with 6.d5!} 5.exd4 5...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight. This is the most popular move, but it allows our d5 idea. After d5, we force the black knight to a bad square. On b4, the knight will be useless because we'll defend d5 right away and the knight will be attacking nothing.} 6.d5 6...Nb8 {If we force the knight back, we can say we have a good position. We'll now build our strong center with 7.c4. Black will have a tough time challenging it. 7...e6 8.Nc3 and the d5-pawn is still holding up strong.} 7.c4 7...g6 {This is one of the few options for the dark-squared bishop. We'll just continue developing with Nc3.} 8.Nc3 8...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. We'll play Bd3, intending to castle next. Our central control is yet to be challenged. Like I said, ...e6 at various points accomplishes very little.} 9.Bd3 9...O-O {Black castles.} 10.O-O {And so do we. We have some plans like Kh1 to get our king off the diagonal, and then develop our pieces around our strong center and dominate the game with our spatial advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's c5 Setups / Study 2: Black plays ...c5 and ...Nf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/413e0f8d-457b-4bd8-971d-7f007239df21/studies/3be57702-33d7-4257-8ef3-3e0dc23be42c/lines"]

1.f4 1...c5 {Black goes for ...c5, controlling the d4-square. We will get the knight out with Nf3...} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {And so does Black, thus controlling each of the central squares with a pawn and a knight respectively. This move is played only 2% of the time in more than 70 000 games. Since we can't play e4, let's go for e3 and fight on the dark squares.} 3.e3 3...g6 {And Black abstains from the early ...Nc6 for a reason. They don't want to run into Bb5 as we've seen in the previous study. As mentioned, this is the most problematic move-order, since there is now no point to Bb5. So we have a new plan. Since we've created a dark-squared pawn chain in the center, we will counter Black's fianchetto with our own.} 4.b3 4...Bg7 {Black develops the bishop. And so will we, of course. In this variation, both sides will develop naturally until a breaking point arrives.} 5.Bb2 5...O-O {Black castles here. As already mentioned, we don't have many squares for the light-squared bishop in this setup. So e2 will suffice.} 6.Be2 6...Nc6 {Finally, once we've committed the bishop, Black goes for ...Nc6. Definitely tricky. We'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...d5 {The active ...d5 is the more popular choice, which you will encounter 44% of the time. This does take central space but the d5-pawn could be a little bit loose if Black doesn't want to protect it with ...e6 and block the bishop. As seen before, we should let Black have the e4-square and play on the dark squares, which is why we will jump into the center right away with Ne5.} 8.Ne5 8...Qc7 {Black will most likely defend with the queen and prepare to recapture. The knight on c6 can be a problematic piece, as we know... So we will get rid of it. There is another idea behind this trade.} 9.Nxc6 9...Qxc6 {And that is, when the queen recaptures...} 10.Bf3 {Now Bf3 is strong, to improve the bishop and target the center. Next, a good idea would be d3+Nd2 to finish development and we've got a healthy, although equal, position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's c5 Setups / Study 2: Black plays ...c5 and ...Nf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/413e0f8d-457b-4bd8-971d-7f007239df21/studies/3be57702-33d7-4257-8ef3-3e0dc23be42c/lines"]

1.f4 1...c5 {Black goes for ...c5, controlling the d4-square. We will get the knight out with Nf3...} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {And so does Black, thus controlling each of the central squares with a pawn and a knight respectively. This move is played only 2% of the time in more than 70 000 games. Since we can't play e4, let's go for e3 and fight on the dark squares.} 3.e3 3...g6 {And Black abstains from the early ...Nc6 for a reason. They don't want to run into Bb5 as we've seen in the previous study. As mentioned, this is the most problematic move-order, since there is now no point to Bb5. So we have a new plan. Since we've created a dark-squared pawn chain in the center, we will counter Black's fianchetto with our own.} 4.b3 4...Bg7 {Black develops the bishop. And so will we, of course. In this variation, both sides will develop naturally until a breaking point arrives.} 5.Bb2 5...O-O {Black castles here. As already mentioned, we don't have many squares for the light-squared bishop in this setup. So e2 will suffice.} 6.Be2 6...Nc6 {Finally, once we've committed the bishop, Black goes for ...Nc6. Definitely tricky. We'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...d6 {A solid setup that counters our dark-squared pawn chain is ...d6, seen 35% of the time. Black simply wants to open up the bishop without worrying about being over-extended in the center. Also, we can't play Ne5 now. Since Black has given us extra space, let's use it with c4. We've got control over both d5 and e5 now and it's not that easy for Black to expand further.} 8.c4 8...a6 {On the other hand, we are the ones that definitely can expand further. And we will do so with d4! Trading in the center and opening up the position will benefit us as we have a little more space. Also, there could be a possibility of trading the knights.} 9.d4 9...cxd4 {Black takes. It's important to keep in mind that we don't want to recapture with the pawn. This could keep our bishop blocked in after ...e6, since we would be reluctant to push d5. We will recapture with the knight instead and keep our bishop active.} 10.Nxd4 10...Nxd4 {When Black captures, we again prefer to avoid capturing with the pawn.} 11.Qxd4 {Taking with the queen is better. Now we even have a strong battery on the dark squares and Black has to be a little bit careful. If Black goes for something like ...Nh5, retreating to d2 is fine since we don't mind trading the bishops.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's c5 Setups / Study 2: Black plays ...c5 and ...Nf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/413e0f8d-457b-4bd8-971d-7f007239df21/studies/3be57702-33d7-4257-8ef3-3e0dc23be42c/lines"]

1.f4 1...c5 {Black goes for ...c5, controlling the d4-square. We will get the knight out with Nf3...} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {And so does Black, thus controlling each of the central squares with a pawn and a knight respectively. This move is played only 2% of the time in more than 70 000 games. Since we can't play e4, let's go for e3 and fight on the dark squares.} 3.e3 3...g6 {And Black abstains from the early ...Nc6 for a reason. They don't want to run into Bb5 as we've seen in the previous study. As mentioned, this is the most problematic move-order, since there is now no point to Bb5. So we have a new plan. Since we've created a dark-squared pawn chain in the center, we will counter Black's fianchetto with our own.} 4.b3 4...Bg7 {Black develops the bishop. And so will we, of course. In this variation, both sides will develop naturally until a breaking point arrives.} 5.Bb2 5...O-O {Black castles here. As already mentioned, we don't have many squares for the light-squared bishop in this setup. So e2 will suffice.} 6.Be2 6...Nc6 {Finally, once we've committed the bishop, Black goes for ...Nc6. Definitely tricky. We'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...d5 {The active ...d5 is the more popular choice, which you will encounter 44% of the time. This does take central space but the d5-pawn could be a little bit loose if Black doesn't want to protect it with ...e6 and block the bishop. As seen before, we should let Black have the e4-square and play on the dark squares, which is why we will jump into the center right away with Ne5.} 8.Ne5 8...Nxe5 {If Black takes...} 9.Bxe5 {It's better to recapture with the bishop and keep it active. Black can't attack it with ...d6. Next, we've got two viable plans: We can either play Nc3 and Bf3 to start targeting the d5-pawn, or we can choose d3 and Nd2 with a more solid approach. Either way, an active kingside idea is Qe1 and Qh4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black's Nf6 and g6 Setups / Study 1: Setups with 1...Nf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/385faf8d-a124-47a9-9ab9-2c841fccfdc1/studies/2edd04ed-7b52-4b07-b19b-93b1d098edca/lines"]

1.f4 1...Nf6 {Black chooses to develop the knight first. This is another way of controlling the center and preventing our e-pawn from advancing to e4. It is played 6% of the time, which is still more than 3 million games in the database. We'll develop the knight behind the pawn. Black will not be playing ...e5 any time soon.} 2.Nf3 2...g6 {The most common move for Black in this position is 2...g6, occurring almost 30% of the time. Already controlling the center, Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. We have already covered 2...e6 within ...d5-e6-Nf6. Since we cannot play e4, I recommend we take control of the d5 in another way, which is c4. The idea is to activate both knights behind the respective pawns.} 3.c4 3...Bg7 {Black goes ahead with their plan... And so will we. Since Black has allowed it, we have strong control over both central squares.} 4.Nc3 4...O-O {A less confrontational move here is castling instead of ...d5. Black wants to give us complete control of the center and undermine our structure at some point. Since the knight is now controlling the e4-square, let's play e4! We are building a huge center.} 5.e4 5...d6 {It is logical for Black to stop e5 with ...d6. Their main ideas will revolve around ...c5 to prevent d4 and fight back in the center. We will activate the bishop in the meantime. We don't have to hurry with a move like d4.} 6.Be2 6...c5 {And if Black goes for this push... Even d3 is completely sufficient, opening up the bishop. We will most likely castle next.} 7.d3 7...Nc6 {Black gets the knight out... And we will castle.} 8.O-O 8...a6 {Another plan that Black can go for is the queenside plan with ...a6. Their main idea is ...Rb8 and ...b5. Since they let us expand in the center, they want the queenside. We can use this time to play the prophylactic Kh1 and get the king away from the dangerous dark diagonal.} 9.Kh1 9...Rb8 {Black supports the push, but we won't let it happen. Let's play a4 and completely lock down the queenside.} 10.a4 10...Nd4 {Black doesn't have many active options now other than centralizing the knight. We will trade in this instance.} 11.Nxd4 11...cxd4 {The pawn recaptures... And we will centralize the knight. The idea is that we want to trade off Black's important kingside defender and push f5 at some point to attack.} 12.Nd5 12...Nxd5 {Black takes. We'll recapture with the c-pawn.} 13.cxd5 13...b5 {Sure, it does allow ...b5 but it keeps our center intact.} 14.a5 {We can push rather than opening up Black's rook and block the file with b4 next move. As mentioned, f5 is coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black's Nf6 and g6 Setups / Study 1: Setups with 1...Nf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/385faf8d-a124-47a9-9ab9-2c841fccfdc1/studies/2edd04ed-7b52-4b07-b19b-93b1d098edca/lines"]

1.f4 1...Nf6 {Black chooses to develop the knight first. This is another way of controlling the center and preventing our e-pawn from advancing to e4. It is played 6% of the time, which is still more than 3 million games in the database. We'll develop the knight behind the pawn. Black will not be playing ...e5 any time soon.} 2.Nf3 2...g6 {The most common move for Black in this position is 2...g6, occurring almost 30% of the time. Already controlling the center, Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. We have already covered 2...e6 within ...d5-e6-Nf6. Since we cannot play e4, I recommend we take control of the d5 in another way, which is c4. The idea is to activate both knights behind the respective pawns.} 3.c4 3...Bg7 {Black goes ahead with their plan... And so will we. Since Black has allowed it, we have strong control over both central squares.} 4.Nc3 4...O-O {A less confrontational move here is castling instead of ...d5. Black wants to give us complete control of the center and undermine our structure at some point. Since the knight is now controlling the e4-square, let's play e4! We are building a huge center.} 5.e4 5...d6 {It is logical for Black to stop e5 with ...d6. Their main ideas will revolve around ...c5 to prevent d4 and fight back in the center. We will activate the bishop in the meantime. We don't have to hurry with a move like d4.} 6.Be2 6...c5 {And if Black goes for this push... Even d3 is completely sufficient, opening up the bishop. We will most likely castle next.} 7.d3 7...Nc6 {Black gets the knight out... And we will castle.} 8.O-O 8...Bg4 {Black could go for further development here, with the plan of capturing on f3 and playing ...Nd4. This is the alternative to going for a queenside plan. We will let them make the trade but prepare for the knight to jump into the center.} 9.Be3 9...Bxf3 {Black takes. We will recapture with the bishop.} 10.Bxf3 10...Nd4 {We don't have to trade again when the knight comes to the center. Instead, a good plan is Rb1 with the idea of b4 to disrupt Black's structure protecting the knight.} 11.Rb1 11...Nxf3+ {This will push Black towards trading on f3 at some point.} 12.Qxf3 {And we will recapture with a now active queen. We have the momentum and will play f5 soon.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black's Nf6 and g6 Setups / Study 1: Setups with 1...Nf6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/385faf8d-a124-47a9-9ab9-2c841fccfdc1/studies/2edd04ed-7b52-4b07-b19b-93b1d098edca/lines"]

1.f4 1...Nf6 {Black chooses to develop the knight first. This is another way of controlling the center and preventing our e-pawn from advancing to e4. It is played 6% of the time, which is still more than 3 million games in the database. We'll develop the knight behind the pawn. Black will not be playing ...e5 any time soon.} 2.Nf3 2...g6 {The most common move for Black in this position is 2...g6, occurring almost 30% of the time. Already controlling the center, Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. We have already covered 2...e6 within ...d5-e6-Nf6. Since we cannot play e4, I recommend we take control of the d5 in another way, which is c4. The idea is to activate both knights behind the respective pawns.} 3.c4 3...Bg7 {Black goes ahead with their plan... And so will we. Since Black has allowed it, we have strong control over both central squares.} 4.Nc3 4...d5 {Although this central move does not lose the game immediately, it leads to tricky positions and might give us a slight advantage in the long run. We will take on d5.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures and attacks f4. But it has left the f6-square, which means that no piece is controlling e4 now. So we will play it! Note that the f4-pawn is hanging.} 6.e4 6...Nxc3 {They can capture the knight here. If we recapture with the b-pawn with hopes of playing d4, Black actually has ...c5. So I recommend recapturing with the d-pawn. This keeps the bishop on g7 restricted and offers a trade of queens.} 7.dxc3 7...Qxd1+ {Black takes. We will recapture.} 8.Kxd1 8...O-O {Sure, Black can castle and we can't. But we have a lot more central space and a lead in activity.} 9.Bc4 9...Rd8+ {Black improves the rook with tempo, but we have a great square for the king to hide on. And that's c2. The king is completely safe and we can continue development.} 10.Kc2 10...Nc6 {Black activates the knight to c6...} 11.Be3 {And we will get the bishop to e3 with a clear plan ahead. That plan is to bring the rooks to the center and expand even further with g4 and h3. I would say that our position is more comfortable.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black's Nf6 and g6 Setups / Study 1: Setups with 1...Nf6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/385faf8d-a124-47a9-9ab9-2c841fccfdc1/studies/2edd04ed-7b52-4b07-b19b-93b1d098edca/lines"]

1.f4 1...Nf6 {Black chooses to develop the knight first. This is another way of controlling the center and preventing our e-pawn from advancing to e4. It is played 6% of the time, which is still more than 3 million games in the database. We'll develop the knight behind the pawn. Black will not be playing ...e5 any time soon.} 2.Nf3 2...b6 {The move 2...b6 is only played 3% of the time, but signifies a specific setup for Black that turns out not to be that bad at all. Black wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and claim an even bigger clamp on e4. We will do the same with our bishop. If Black wants the e4-square, we will get the e5-square.} 3.b3 3...Bb7 {They choose to fianchetto now... And so will we. It is almost a symmetrical position, with the exception that we have a pawn on f4 that covers e5 as well. When Black opts for this line, it is important to know our plan from the start. It is to develop the bishops, play Rg1 at some point and attack the king.} 4.Bb2 4...e6 {Black opens up their bishop... And we will do the same with ours, connecting the pawn chain.} 5.e3 5...Be7 {Now Black is ready to castle. Keep in mind that it is possible for you to play Rg1 even here, but Black has not castled yet. And an early Rg1 might give them second thoughts about doing so. So I suggest we just develop with Bd3.} 6.Bd3 6...O-O {And once Black castles... Let Rg1 come as a sort of a surprise. We want to push the g-pawn next and attack. You will notice that our king will actually be safe in the center.} 7.Rg1 7...d5 {Black has to play actively, so they try to counter with ...d5. It does grab space, but blocks the bishop and doesn't do anything about our plans. Black probably wants to centralize the knight. We will push! g5 is coming.} 8.g4 8...Ne4 {Especially if the knight jumps to e4. We don't want Black to infiltrate with a move like ...Bh4. So we will play g5 and block the bishop. Now Black should probably continue with a solid move like ...Nd7.} 9.g5 9...c5 {But ...c5 looks much more active. It is a mistake, though. Because it allows this trade from us, with an easy plan to attack afterwards.} 10.Bxe4 10...dxe4 {Black recaptures.} 11.Ne5 {And now we can centralize our own knight on e5, resembling lines analyzed earlier in this chapter. From there, it can jump to g4 and control some key dark squares near Black's king. Our queen can join the attack on h5 and we will get the rest of the pieces out. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black's Nf6 and g6 Setups / Study 1: Setups with 1...Nf6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/385faf8d-a124-47a9-9ab9-2c841fccfdc1/studies/2edd04ed-7b52-4b07-b19b-93b1d098edca/lines"]

1.f4 1...Nf6 {Black chooses to develop the knight first. This is another way of controlling the center and preventing our e-pawn from advancing to e4. It is played 6% of the time, which is still more than 3 million games in the database. We'll develop the knight behind the pawn. Black will not be playing ...e5 any time soon.} 2.Nf3 2...b6 {The move 2...b6 is only played 3% of the time, but signifies a specific setup for Black that turns out not to be that bad at all. Black wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and claim an even bigger clamp on e4. We will do the same with our bishop. If Black wants the e4-square, we will get the e5-square.} 3.b3 3...Bb7 {They choose to fianchetto now... And so will we. It is almost a symmetrical position, with the exception that we have a pawn on f4 that covers e5 as well. When Black opts for this line, it is important to know our plan from the start. It is to develop the bishops, play Rg1 at some point and attack the king.} 4.Bb2 4...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto on g7 as well. This is a dynamic and fun line. With the double fianchetto, Black hopes to exert pressure along both diagonals. We will play our normal move to support our forward f4-pawn.} 5.e3 5...Bg7 {And Black will fianchetto. We now have a chance of doing something interesting. With 5...Bg7, Black got themselves in a pin. They cannot take on g4 so we push it!} 6.g4 6...O-O {Black castles. They can now take on g4 because the bishop is defended. But we'll push! And just like that we have a space advantage on the kingside, and we will trade one of Black's important king defenders.} 7.g5 7...Nh5 {The knight goes to h5. We'll trade.} 8.Bxg7 8...Nxg7 {They recapture. And we'll fianchetto our light-squared bishop. We aren't allowing Black to keep complete control of any diagonal.} 9.Bg2 9...d6 {A normal developing move is 9...d6. Black wants to get the knight out and also prepare a potential expansion like ...e5.} 10.Qe2 {We have a fantastic plan of h4, h5 to open up the h-file. We will of course first play Nc3 and 0-0-0 so that our pieces can help us in our attack of the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black's Nf6 and g6 Setups / Study 1: Setups with 1...Nf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/385faf8d-a124-47a9-9ab9-2c841fccfdc1/studies/2edd04ed-7b52-4b07-b19b-93b1d098edca/lines"]

1.f4 1...Nf6 {Black chooses to develop the knight first. This is another way of controlling the center and preventing our e-pawn from advancing to e4. It is played 6% of the time, which is still more than 3 million games in the database. We'll develop the knight behind the pawn. Black will not be playing ...e5 any time soon.} 2.Nf3 2...g6 {The most common move for Black in this position is 2...g6, occurring almost 30% of the time. Already controlling the center, Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. We have already covered 2...e6 within ...d5-e6-Nf6. Since we cannot play e4, I recommend we take control of the d5 in another way, which is c4. The idea is to activate both knights behind the respective pawns.} 3.c4 3...Bg7 {Black goes ahead with their plan... And so will we. Since Black has allowed it, we have strong control over both central squares.} 4.Nc3 4...d5 {Although this central move does not lose the game immediately, it leads to tricky positions and might give us a slight advantage in the long run. We will take on d5.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures and attacks f4. But it has left the f6-square, which means that no piece is controlling e4 now. So we will play it! Note that the f4-pawn is hanging.} 6.e4 6...Nxf4 {Taking the pawn is not a blunder, but gives us a lot of space and a comfortable position. We now get to place both pawns in the center with tempo. Black's knight is under attack.} 7.d4 7...Ne6 {Black will most likely retreat to e6. The d4-pawn is under attack, and we don't want to push d5 and allow ...Nc5 with further pressure. So we will simply protect the pawn.} 8.Be3 8...O-O {Once Black castles, now it is okay to push d5, as there is no ...Nc5. So we will play it! If you take a look at the position, Black actually doesn't have a free square for the knight.} 9.d5 9...Bxc3+ {The only way to save it is this trade. We will recapture.} 10.bxc3 10...Ng7 {And now the knight gets the square on g7. But trading off the strong dark-squared bishop like this and leaving us with a bishop pair is probably not optimal for Black. We will continue naturally, getting the queen out.} 11.Qd2 11...e6 {Black can try to undermine our structure if they like.} 12.Bc4 {But we can develop and defend. Castling is next. As mentioned, we have more central space, a lead in activity and the bishop pair: good compensation for the pawn. The position is still equal if Black plays correctly, however.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black's Nf6 and g6 Setups / Study 1: Setups with 1...Nf6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/385faf8d-a124-47a9-9ab9-2c841fccfdc1/studies/2edd04ed-7b52-4b07-b19b-93b1d098edca/lines"]

1.f4 1...Nf6 {Black chooses to develop the knight first. This is another way of controlling the center and preventing our e-pawn from advancing to e4. It is played 6% of the time, which is still more than 3 million games in the database. We'll develop the knight behind the pawn. Black will not be playing ...e5 any time soon.} 2.Nf3 2...g6 {The most common move for Black in this position is 2...g6, occurring almost 30% of the time. Already controlling the center, Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. We have already covered 2...e6 within ...d5-e6-Nf6. Since we cannot play e4, I recommend we take control of the d5 in another way, which is c4. The idea is to activate both knights behind the respective pawns.} 3.c4 3...Bg7 {Black goes ahead with their plan... And so will we. Since Black has allowed it, we have strong control over both central squares.} 4.Nc3 4...d5 {Although this central move does not lose the game immediately, it leads to tricky positions and might give us a slight advantage in the long run. We will take on d5.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures and attacks f4. But it has left the f6-square, which means that no piece is controlling e4 now. So we will play it! Note that the f4-pawn is hanging.} 6.e4 6...Nxf4 {Taking the pawn is not a blunder, but gives us a lot of space and a comfortable position. We now get to place both pawns in the center with tempo. Black's knight is under attack.} 7.d4 7...Nh5 {Going to h5 is passive. We will just get the bishop out.} 8.Be2 8...O-O {Black castles...} 9.O-O {And so will we. The bishop is coming out next and the knight is still weak, stuck on the edge of the board. Our compensation lies in activity and the big center.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black's Nf6 and g6 Setups / Study 2: Setups with 1...g6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/385faf8d-a124-47a9-9ab9-2c841fccfdc1/studies/915313c1-0355-46de-98d1-af3eabee3a84/lines"]

1.f4 1...g6 {And Black goes for the immediate ...g6. These kinds of setups can be played against basically any opening, but Black does cede the center. It is played 5% of the time, amounting to more than 2.3 million games. We will get the knight out. Our main idea is to go for e4 since Black has allowed it.} 2.Nf3 2...Bg7 {And if they fianchetto... That is exactly what we will play.} 3.e4 3...d6 {The most common move after 3.e4 is ...d6, played 37% of the time. Black wants to play King's Indian style. That means activating the kingside quickly and fighting back with ...e5 or ...c5. Since Black has allowed it, let's take the center! We've got three strong pawns controlling it.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Instead of activating the knight, Black can choose to get the bishop out and pin our knight on f3. This could be useful if we are not careful, as Black wants to capture on f3 while attacking d4. So we will defend the center. They can capture on f3 now all they want.} 5.c3 5...Nd7 {They opt to develop instead... And we will attack the bishop on g4. Since the knight on d7 is blocking the bishop from retreating all the way, Black doesn't have many options here.} 6.h3 6...Bxf3 {So they capture. We will take back with the queen, of course.} 7.Qxf3 7...e5 {Once ...e5 arrives, no panic. Capturing on e5 only opens up the position for Black. Our center is already powerful and we should keep it as such. So we will protect the center calmly with Be3.} 8.Be3 8...Ne7 {Black activates the other knight. Although they are going to castle next move, their pieces aren't exactly active. Meanwhile we will look to castle long.} 9.Nd2 9...O-O {Black gets the king to safety...} 10.O-O-O {And so do we. We castled long because we want to use our central and kingside space to expand and attack further. This is the easiest with moves like g4 and h4. We have got some advantage already.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black's Nf6 and g6 Setups / Study 2: Setups with 1...g6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/385faf8d-a124-47a9-9ab9-2c841fccfdc1/studies/915313c1-0355-46de-98d1-af3eabee3a84/lines"]

1.f4 1...g6 {And Black goes for the immediate ...g6. These kinds of setups can be played against basically any opening, but Black does cede the center. It is played 5% of the time, amounting to more than 2.3 million games. We will get the knight out. Our main idea is to go for e4 since Black has allowed it.} 2.Nf3 2...Bg7 {And if they fianchetto... That is exactly what we will play.} 3.e4 3...e6 {3...e6 is played 23% of the time, and comes with the clear intention of supporting the ...d5 push. However, Black gives us the center completely after d4.} 4.d4 4...Ne7 {On the other hand, they want to stay solid and castle, while aiming to strike back with both ...d5 and ...c5 at some point. We will continue in a natural manner, developing our bishop and preparing to castle ourselves.} 5.Bd3 5...O-O {Black castles. And so do we.} 6.O-O 6...d5 {Expect to see ...d5 at some point. Black's ...e6 move supports it after all. We don't want to capture and open up the position in Black's favor. It's better to open up our bishop while grabbing space with e5.} 7.e5 7...c5 {And if Black continues undermining our structure, we abstained from playing Nc3 for a reason.} 8.c3 {We need the c3-square to defend the center and create this beautiful and strong pawn structure. White is already slightly better. We'll get the bishop out to e3 and could even have plans of pushing g4 at some point.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black's Nf6 and g6 Setups / Study 2: Setups with 1...g6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/29efa96f-3cf6-4f49-80be-57eb3c015e0e/chapters/385faf8d-a124-47a9-9ab9-2c841fccfdc1/studies/915313c1-0355-46de-98d1-af3eabee3a84/lines"]

1.f4 1...g6 {And Black goes for the immediate ...g6. These kinds of setups can be played against basically any opening, but Black does cede the center. It is played 5% of the time, amounting to more than 2.3 million games. We will get the knight out. Our main idea is to go for e4 since Black has allowed it.} 2.Nf3 2...Bg7 {And if they fianchetto... That is exactly what we will play.} 3.e4 3...d6 {The most common move after 3.e4 is ...d6, played 37% of the time. Black wants to play King's Indian style. That means activating the kingside quickly and fighting back with ...e5 or ...c5. Since Black has allowed it, let's take the center! We've got three strong pawns controlling it.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {When Black activates the knight and attacks the e4-pawn, we don't have to protect it. We can push it right away! This is one of the benefits of having a pawn on f4 early. It might seem like it is dangerous to push this far this early, but Black is the one who will have to move a piece more times in the opening.} 5.e5 5...dxe5 {If Black takes... We will recapture with the f-pawn. This avoids the queen trade and opens up our bishop.} 6.fxe5 6...Nd5 {If Black places the knight in the center, don't be afraid to attack it again. This is not overextending as we are building a big center and will reinforce it with a lot of pieces to come.} 7.c4 7...Nb6 {Black escapes to b6. Our knight on f3 is our only developed piece and holds our position together. We must not allow ...Bg4 to pin it. So we will play h3. Now our important defender can't be attacked easily.} 8.h3 8...O-O {Black castles...} 9.Nc3 {And we will get the second knight out. We did push a lot of pawns in the opening, but Black didn't have any concrete counterplay and we will manage to consolidate very soon. Namely with moves like Be3, Be2 and O-O. White is better.} *